00:01:37.610 --> 00:01:40.380
What you’re looking at is Decentraland.

00:01:40.380 --> 00:01:44.700
Conceived by Argentine developers Esteban
Ordano and Ari Meilich as a virtual reality

00:01:44.700 --> 00:01:50.360
world for unconstrained markets and presently
advertising itself as a “decentralized metaverse

00:01:50.360 --> 00:01:52.520
owned by its users.”

00:01:52.520 --> 00:01:58.570
In the pitch version Decentraland is a true
virtual space, a whole new parallel world

00:01:58.570 --> 00:02:04.469
that you access via virtual reality, a world
where you can shop, buy things, invest, purchase

00:02:04.469 --> 00:02:09.140
goods, and go to stores unconstrained by the
laws that govern the place where your physical

00:02:09.140 --> 00:02:15.849
body eats and sleeps, a product so promising
that it commands a valuation in the billions

00:02:15.849 --> 00:02:16.879
of dollars.

00:02:16.879 --> 00:02:22.170
In practice, it’s just a bad video game
made up of smaller, worse video games, wrapped

00:02:22.170 --> 00:02:25.879
in a real-estate scheme cosplaying as the
Matrix.

00:02:25.879 --> 00:02:30.280
Most of what Decentraland does, and what it
fails to do, are things that would be considered

00:02:30.280 --> 00:02:34.519
forgivable or quaint in a Kickstarter MMO
that had clearly bitten off more than the

00:02:34.519 --> 00:02:40.080
creators could ever chew, but given that this
is a project founded on cryptocurrency all

00:02:40.080 --> 00:02:44.140
of those foibles are laced with the language
of finance and landlordism.

00:02:44.140 --> 00:02:48.760
Strolling down Decentraland’s spacious boulevards
at 5 frames per second rewards the user with

00:02:48.760 --> 00:02:53.750
a seemingly endless parade of virtual billboards
brightly proclaiming that the space you see

00:02:53.750 --> 00:02:55.959
is all available to rent.

00:02:55.959 --> 00:03:00.510
This is because of a pretty basic chain of
incentives that are laid bare by Decentraland’s

00:03:00.510 --> 00:03:01.510
history.

00:03:01.510 --> 00:03:05.709
While the nominally-playable game launched
in February 2020 the actual launch was in

00:03:05.709 --> 00:03:11.569
2017 broken into two parts: first an initial
coin offering in August where their MANA token

00:03:11.569 --> 00:03:17.610
sale raised approximately $24 million dollars
denominated in crypto coins, and second with

00:03:17.610 --> 00:03:22.210
the “terraforming event” where parcels
of land, denominated in LAND tokens, were

00:03:22.210 --> 00:03:27.280
auctioned off for an additional $28 million
in crypto, predominantly bought by guys like

00:03:27.280 --> 00:03:28.280
this.

00:03:28.280 --> 00:03:30.850
“I’m not selling until it’s worth at
least ten million”

00:03:30.850 --> 00:03:35.260
This being crypto the obsession with scarcity
is so pervasive that it’s almost a waste

00:03:35.260 --> 00:03:41.129
of time to even mention it but the LAND tokens
were limited to 90,000, which, of course,

00:03:41.129 --> 00:03:45.269
centered a lot of the discussion of Decentraland
around the hypothetical value of the virtual

00:03:45.269 --> 00:03:51.010
plots of land in the event that Decentraland
won the future and became the Manhattan of

00:03:51.010 --> 00:03:54.989
the metaverse, the go-to destination for virtual
life.

00:03:54.989 --> 00:03:59.650
The initial buyers' plans for the land overwhelmingly
veered towards pitching cryptocurrency or

00:03:59.650 --> 00:04:04.680
financial services or just sitting on the
land with the intent of renting it out later.

00:04:04.680 --> 00:04:09.010
This is all baked-in, Decentraland’s initial
pitch hinges on it.

00:04:09.010 --> 00:04:13.120
“What if you could own the virtual world?

00:04:13.120 --> 00:04:17.959
Create, develop, and trade without limits.

00:04:17.959 --> 00:04:21.700
Make genuine connections, and earn real money.

00:04:21.700 --> 00:04:22.700
Decentraland.

00:04:22.700 --> 00:04:27.670
A fully immersive platform powered by the
blockchain.

00:04:27.670 --> 00:04:30.250
Buy land.

00:04:30.250 --> 00:04:34.120
Design your experience.

00:04:34.120 --> 00:04:38.530
And transform he way that people see the world.

00:04:38.530 --> 00:04:42.880
Purchase the first ever virtual real estate.

00:04:42.880 --> 00:04:46.630
Get started today.”

00:04:46.630 --> 00:04:52.440
Now, there’s a lot of ground that needs
to be covered, and a not insubstantial amount

00:04:52.440 --> 00:04:55.880
of theory that I would like to get out there,
but I suspect the main thing you might be

00:04:55.880 --> 00:05:01.480
here for is failure, so just to whet your
appetite here is a list of things you can

00:05:01.480 --> 00:05:06.699
expect to see as we talk about Decentraland,
what it is, and what it isn’t.

00:05:06.699 --> 00:05:09.770
[Disco Music]&nbsp;

00:05:09.770 --> 00:05:13.840
Decentraland Records presents the only collection
of failure you’ll ever need!

00:05:13.840 --> 00:05:17.190
Bad graphics
Poor performance

00:05:17.190 --> 00:05:19.470
Dead mall vibes
Missing geometry

00:05:19.470 --> 00:05:22.270
Weed leaves
Loading boxes

00:05:22.270 --> 00:05:23.789
And so much more

00:05:23.789 --> 00:05:44.740
But before we can get into all that we’ve
gotta talk about the metaverse.

00:05:44.740 --> 00:05:48.820
In the past two years you have likely been
inundated with attempts to explain and define

00:05:48.820 --> 00:05:53.780
the metaverse and yet, despite the numerous,
numerous attempts you probably feel no closer

00:05:53.780 --> 00:05:57.930
to having an actual, workable definition in
your head, one that maps to something you

00:05:57.930 --> 00:06:00.810
can visualize actually integrating with your
life.

00:06:00.810 --> 00:06:04.440
There are a lot of definitions out there,
most of them are incredibly flimsy, many of

00:06:04.440 --> 00:06:09.410
them are outright insane, very few agree on
anything other than the broad strokes, and

00:06:09.410 --> 00:06:13.400
almost all of them fall back on gesturing
towards Ready Player One and going “you

00:06:13.400 --> 00:06:16.510
know, that thing, it’ll be like that.”

00:06:16.510 --> 00:06:21.360
In the mainstream, the metaverse has been
somewhat hijacked by the crypto and web3 crowd.

00:06:21.360 --> 00:06:25.550
An interesting element to that is that while
they are extremely aggressively pushing the

00:06:25.550 --> 00:06:30.680
word “metaverse” out into the public consciousness,
they are late-arriving parasites, they will

00:06:30.680 --> 00:06:34.870
typically nod their heads at any metaverse
vision before throwing it back at you with

00:06:34.870 --> 00:06:36.990
their crypto junk grafted onto it.

00:06:36.990 --> 00:06:42.050
They don’t care about the form or content
of the metaverse, but both crypto and the

00:06:42.050 --> 00:06:46.780
metaverse require spontaneous mass adoption
to make sense, so the two are somewhat simpatico

00:06:46.780 --> 00:06:48.139
in that regard.

00:06:48.139 --> 00:06:50.900
In sharp contrast we have the writing of Ryan
Bolger.

00:06:50.900 --> 00:06:56.010
A theologian, Bolger’s vision of the Metaverse
is that of a reified space that sits on top

00:06:56.010 --> 00:06:59.449
of the real world, a 1 to 1 digital clone.

00:06:59.449 --> 00:07:03.970
You can leave a digital letter for someone
on a park bench, and they need to go to that

00:07:03.970 --> 00:07:05.990
same bench to find it.

00:07:05.990 --> 00:07:11.690
A clone so accurate that self-driving cars
will navigate not via sensors that monitor

00:07:11.690 --> 00:07:17.420
the world around them, but by referencing
the current position of things in the metaverse

00:07:17.420 --> 00:07:20.960
- the most jacked version of Google Maps you
can imagine.&nbsp;

00:07:20.960 --> 00:07:25.750
For Bolger, traditional websites and apps
are just a stop gap to the ultimate aim, which

00:07:25.750 --> 00:07:31.550
should be to astrally project via VR into
a McDonalds, where a cashier will then see

00:07:31.550 --> 00:07:36.350
you through AR glasses and take your order
as if you were in the space.

00:07:36.350 --> 00:07:41.920
The goal of this degree of reification was
to create a space so ‘real’ and legitimate,

00:07:41.920 --> 00:07:47.830
that it could be said that the metaverse could
be capable of containing divinity - a church

00:07:47.830 --> 00:07:52.729
in the metaverse can be sacred independent
of the context of human interaction because

00:07:52.729 --> 00:07:56.920
it’s not just software, it’s a place.

00:07:56.920 --> 00:08:01.940
Digital fashion and other “phygital” industries
also want to break down the barrier between

00:08:01.940 --> 00:08:05.389
physical and digital, but for very different
reasons.&nbsp;

00:08:05.389 --> 00:08:09.300
They look at the market for cosmetics in live
service video games and crave translating

00:08:09.300 --> 00:08:11.590
that business model to reality.

00:08:11.590 --> 00:08:15.800
Rather than building a reified virtual world,
digital fashion is much more interested in

00:08:15.800 --> 00:08:20.569
the augmented reality side of things, they
want AR glasses that are so universal and

00:08:20.569 --> 00:08:25.220
so seamless that we all dress in mo-cap suits
and composite the new Gucci over it using

00:08:25.220 --> 00:08:27.960
an advanced spin on Tiktok’s hat filter.

00:08:27.960 --> 00:08:32.550
In this definition the metaverse isn’t another
place, conceptual or otherwise, it’s just

00:08:32.550 --> 00:08:37.039
a name for the digital skin that gets laid
over the world when viewed through compatible

00:08:37.039 --> 00:08:38.190
devices.

00:08:38.190 --> 00:08:41.490
We strongly believe that the amount of clothing
produced today is way greater than humanity

00:08:41.490 --> 00:08:42.490
needs.

00:08:42.490 --> 00:08:45.970
But don’t shop less, shop digital fashion.

00:08:45.970 --> 00:08:47.880
Don’t shop less.

00:08:47.880 --> 00:08:49.230
Don’t shop less.

00:08:49.230 --> 00:08:55.950
Don’t, don’t you, don’t you dare, don’t
you [static] dare shop less, don’t you

00:08:55.950 --> 00:09:00.230
There are numerous problems here, which boil
down to the fact that all of these have equal

00:09:00.230 --> 00:09:02.839
claim to the word “metaverse.”

00:09:02.839 --> 00:09:07.940
If you comb through dozens and dozens of definitions
of the metaverse you can assemble a web of

00:09:07.940 --> 00:09:13.450
broad attributes where some are generally
agreed upon while others border on being mutually

00:09:13.450 --> 00:09:14.450
exclusive.

00:09:14.450 --> 00:09:19.600
It’s a vague, largely incoherent cloud of
ideas that’s malleable enough that basically

00:09:19.600 --> 00:09:26.720
anything can be called part of the metaverse,
a proto-metaverse, or a semi-metaverse.

00:09:26.720 --> 00:09:32.090
Decentraland is the metaverse, TheSandbox
is the metaverse, Horizon Worlds is the metaverse,

00:09:32.090 --> 00:09:36.740
Roblox is the metaverse, Fortnite is the metaverse,
Minecraft is the metaverse.

00:09:36.740 --> 00:09:39.970
The Lindt Experience is the metaverse.

00:09:39.970 --> 00:09:43.680
“I look at my fourteen year old and she
has spent the last year and a half of her

00:09:43.680 --> 00:09:44.880
life living in a metaverse.

00:09:44.880 --> 00:09:50.110
She doesn’t even know that word, right,
but her school is on Zoom, she hangs out with

00:09:50.110 --> 00:09:53.940
her friends online in one form or another,
and actually in a whole bunch of different

00:09:53.940 --> 00:09:54.940
forms, right.

00:09:54.940 --> 00:10:01.530
In Instagram, in TikTok, in iMessage, in,
in Fortnite, in Animal Crossing, in, uh, you

00:10:01.530 --> 00:10:04.970
know all of these different, you know, metaverses,
right”

00:10:04.970 --> 00:10:09.080
“Some leaders within the virtual worlds
space, such as Tim Sweeney, believe that eventually,

00:10:09.080 --> 00:10:13.029
every company will need to operate their own
virtual worlds, both as standalone planets

00:10:13.029 --> 00:10:16.709
and as part of leading virtual world platforms
such as Fortnite and Minecraft.

00:10:16.709 --> 00:10:21.160
As Sweeney has put it, “just as every company
a few decades ago created a webpage, and then

00:10:21.160 --> 00:10:24.410
at some point every company created a Facebook
page.”

00:10:24.410 --> 00:10:29.130
Of course this flexibility that allows for
anything good and popular to be part of or

00:10:29.130 --> 00:10:34.720
a natural, inevitable precursor to the True
Metaverse simultaneously provides the flexibility

00:10:34.720 --> 00:10:40.730
to dismiss any failure as a failing of that
pure vision rather than a failure of the underlying

00:10:40.730 --> 00:10:42.380
ideas themselves.

00:10:42.380 --> 00:10:47.470
The metaverse cannot fail, you can only fail
to make the metaverse.

00:10:47.470 --> 00:10:51.930
There’s no consensus definition because
the Metaverse isn’t real, it’s not an

00:10:51.930 --> 00:10:56.779
actual thing that anyone’s really building,
it’s a rhetorical device that allows the

00:10:56.779 --> 00:11:02.149
writer to gesture at the future and pontificate
on what ought to be.

00:11:02.149 --> 00:11:06.190
Definitions of the metaverse are, thusly,
principally rooted in the writer’s present

00:11:06.190 --> 00:11:08.450
political and social values.

00:11:08.450 --> 00:11:12.890
When you understand that the metaverse isn’t
a distinct invention or construct but merely

00:11:12.890 --> 00:11:19.470
a rhetorical proxy for The Future of Technology
then all of this becomes a lot easier to deal

00:11:19.470 --> 00:11:20.470
with.

00:11:20.470 --> 00:11:24.459
The reason it’s all so messy is because
of the aforementioned gesturing towards fiction

00:11:24.459 --> 00:11:29.720
and because a lot of the people writing effluent
prose about how the future is here today in

00:11:29.720 --> 00:11:35.200
the form of blockchain-based social spaces
are, for the most part, salesmen.

00:11:35.200 --> 00:11:39.810
That’s not to say that they don’t believe
the Metaverse could or should exist, just

00:11:39.810 --> 00:11:44.460
that most have not considered if it would
actually be any good.

00:11:44.460 --> 00:11:49.529
That part they take as a given, because it’s
always super cool in stories, so why even

00:11:49.529 --> 00:11:54.970
bother considering that the end result might
be boring, inconvenient, and disappointing?

00:11:54.970 --> 00:11:59.190
Now, science fiction and actual science have
a long and storied history.&nbsp;

00:11:59.190 --> 00:12:03.470
It’s fun to point to all the devices that
exist today that were first sketched out by

00:12:03.470 --> 00:12:08.470
a fiction writer decades earlier, but there’s
also a massive survivorship bias there.

00:12:08.470 --> 00:12:12.870
Sure, some speculations become real, either
because the author rooted their speculation

00:12:12.870 --> 00:12:17.070
in cutting edge science or because fans of
the work tried extremely hard to make it a

00:12:17.070 --> 00:12:22.370
reality, but even more get discarded as silly,
useless, impractical, deadly, or physically

00:12:22.370 --> 00:12:23.770
impossible.

00:12:23.770 --> 00:12:29.200
This might come as a shock, but when a writer
sits down to compose fiction, the thing that

00:12:29.200 --> 00:12:32.500
they write doesn’t need to actually work.

00:12:32.500 --> 00:12:37.570
They’re not real inventions, they’re narrative
devices, and at a certain point no amount

00:12:37.570 --> 00:12:41.540
of cool factor can make up for the limitations
of physical space.

00:12:41.540 --> 00:12:47.420
Star Trek’s holodeck is an insanely cool
idea, and dear god do I wish it were real.

00:12:47.420 --> 00:12:52.210
But it can’t exist, not as depicted, because
it’s not an invention, it’s a collection

00:12:52.210 --> 00:12:54.770
of camera tricks and special effects.

00:12:54.770 --> 00:13:00.279
At the end of the day the holodeck’s limitations,
and lack thereof, are dictated by narrative

00:13:00.279 --> 00:13:01.810
necessity.

00:13:01.810 --> 00:13:07.680
So when Neal Stephenson writes about The Metaverse
in Snow Crash in 1992 it is an invention composed

00:13:07.680 --> 00:13:12.810
of a mix of speculation about the future of
existing hardware, trends in how people interact

00:13:12.810 --> 00:13:19.520
via virtual spaces, and an overwhelmingly
cartoonish dose of Rule of Cool.

00:13:19.520 --> 00:13:25.550
Stephenson’s Metaverse is a massive, three-dimensional
contiguous space because it’s evocative

00:13:25.550 --> 00:13:31.290
and interesting, it provides a colourful backdrop
for the story’s gonzo plot about a high

00:13:31.290 --> 00:13:36.740
stakes pizza delivery swordmaster employed
by the mafia and a computer virus that can

00:13:36.740 --> 00:13:38.440
cause brain damage.

00:13:38.440 --> 00:13:43.620
Snow Crash is prescient in degrees about some
of the ways in which we would engage with

00:13:43.620 --> 00:13:49.700
technology, but overwhelmingly that prescience
applies to the internet as it currently exists.

00:13:49.700 --> 00:13:56.130
We have virtual live events, virtual sports,
virtual town squares, virtual club houses,

00:13:56.130 --> 00:14:00.769
and even virtual strip clubs where you can
hang out in chat with five hundred strangers

00:14:00.769 --> 00:14:05.620
and watch someone just absolutely [static].

00:14:05.620 --> 00:14:10.029
Really the only things that are missing are
either aesthetic, like the giant contiguous

00:14:10.029 --> 00:14:14.820
three dimensional representation of the internet
where you can walk from one webpage to the

00:14:14.820 --> 00:14:21.510
next, or maybe not even physically possible
at all, like the Snow Crash virus itself.

00:14:21.510 --> 00:14:25.899
So this then creates a bit of a trap for a
lot of the salesmen writing about the inevitability

00:14:25.899 --> 00:14:31.300
of the metaverse where they have to cling
to those aesthetic details as non-negotiable

00:14:31.300 --> 00:14:35.220
because otherwise they’re just talking about
the internet.

00:14:35.220 --> 00:14:39.870
As Matthew Ball writes in his 2022 book The
Metaverse and How It Will Change Everything

00:14:39.870 --> 00:14:44.981
“Although virtual worlds come in many dimensions,
“3D” is a critical specification for the

00:14:44.981 --> 00:14:45.981
Metaverse.

00:14:45.981 --> 00:14:48.769
Without 3D, we might as well be describing
the current internet.

00:14:48.769 --> 00:14:52.709
Message boards, chat services, website builders,
image platforms, and interconnected networks

00:14:52.709 --> 00:14:56.600
of content have been around and popular for
decades, after all.”

00:14:56.600 --> 00:14:59.300
Which brings us to virtual reality.

00:14:59.300 --> 00:15:02.880
VR is pretty cool tech, I really like it.

00:15:02.880 --> 00:15:07.850
It’s also pretty limited, or more specifically
the tech runs up against a pretty big barrier

00:15:07.850 --> 00:15:10.010
which is the human body.

00:15:10.010 --> 00:15:14.130
The inner ear really doesn’t like moving
without actually moving, which means that

00:15:14.130 --> 00:15:19.820
every VR application is a series of compromises
between the immersive potential of VR and

00:15:19.820 --> 00:15:21.880
what the body will tolerate.

00:15:21.880 --> 00:15:22.880
No!

00:15:22.880 --> 00:15:23.880
Oh god!

00:15:23.880 --> 00:15:24.880
Oh god!

00:15:24.880 --> 00:15:25.880
Oh!

00:15:25.880 --> 00:15:29.959
Because Stephenson’s metaverse is a plot
device, effectively an alternate dimension,

00:15:29.959 --> 00:15:34.190
the characters are able to navigate the space
however Stephenson wants them to.

00:15:34.190 --> 00:15:38.310
And because it’s narratively interesting,
when the characters want to get from one part

00:15:38.310 --> 00:15:43.010
of the metaverse to another they need to traverse
the distance between those two points.

00:15:43.010 --> 00:15:47.250
Ball, for his part, concedes that users of
the inevitable metaverse would likely find

00:15:47.250 --> 00:15:52.029
this to be too inconvenient and prefer to
teleport from location to location.

00:15:52.029 --> 00:15:55.139
The reality is even more restrictive.&nbsp;

00:15:55.139 --> 00:16:01.390
The compromise is that overwhelmingly virtual
reality experiences are largely static experiences,

00:16:01.390 --> 00:16:06.290
with freeform movement around a small footprint,
with the user teleporting from spot to spot

00:16:06.290 --> 00:16:08.600
even within the same virtual room.

00:16:08.600 --> 00:16:13.660
It’s a compromise that VR enthusiasts are
willing to accept because the alternative

00:16:13.660 --> 00:16:19.329
is a one-way trip to the floor as your cochlea
decides that being upright is no longer an

00:16:19.329 --> 00:16:20.329
option.

00:16:20.329 --> 00:16:26.000
The end result, the actuality of virtual reality,
is that it’s an incredibly potent tool for

00:16:26.000 --> 00:16:29.420
an unfortunately limited number of applications.&nbsp;

00:16:29.420 --> 00:16:34.040
This creates a very sharp contrast between
the metaverse that’s talked about by pundits

00:16:34.040 --> 00:16:39.290
and the reality of VR that developers and
enthusiasts have been mapping for over a decade,

00:16:39.290 --> 00:16:45.100
with pundits continuing to insist that a vast,
contiguous space representing the internet

00:16:45.100 --> 00:16:51.620
is not only a desirable way to interact with
those functionalities, but indeed a preferential

00:16:51.620 --> 00:16:52.620
way.

00:16:52.620 --> 00:16:57.800
How this manifests is a metaverse that exists
overwhelmingly in the form of blog posts about

00:16:57.800 --> 00:17:04.130
the inevitability of its existence, with anything
tangentially related to virtual reality being

00:17:04.130 --> 00:17:09.839
encircled as part of the metaverse, along
with a whole lot of stuff hardly related at

00:17:09.839 --> 00:17:10.839
all.

00:17:10.839 --> 00:17:14.980
Of particular fascination is the way that
this is all talk and no product.

00:17:14.980 --> 00:17:20.380
Lindt USA, manufacturers of terrible chocolate
truffles, launched their metaverse store in

00:17:20.380 --> 00:17:25.290
November 2022 and just have a look at the
language used to describe it.

00:17:25.290 --> 00:17:32.080
Lindt USA Launches Its First-Ever 3D Virtual
Store with ByondXR and Makes Shopping for

00:17:32.080 --> 00:17:36.840
Premium Chocolate a Truly Innovative Experience

00:17:36.840 --> 00:17:42.280
In a beautifully decorated, fully 3D and highly
photorealistic environment, the store welcomes

00:17:42.280 --> 00:17:47.620
visitors with an engaging introduction by
one of Lindt’s famous master chocolatiers,

00:17:47.620 --> 00:17:48.820
Ann Czaja.

00:17:48.820 --> 00:17:51.950
Best of all, the immersive store is totally
comprehensible:

00:17:51.950 --> 00:17:56.960
“We at Lindt USA are thrilled to offer customers
this state-of-the-art experience just in time

00:17:56.960 --> 00:17:58.110
for the holidays.

00:17:58.110 --> 00:18:00.830
While there is no experience like walking
into one of our beautiful Lindt stores in

00:18:00.830 --> 00:18:03.570
person, our online store is no different.

00:18:03.570 --> 00:18:05.429
We’re excited to show it off.”&nbsp;

00:18:05.429 --> 00:18:10.230
This is all pretty routine corporate fluff,
though the surprising, and telling thing is

00:18:10.230 --> 00:18:15.900
that it’s just words, there’s few, if
any, images included.

00:18:15.900 --> 00:18:21.700
And there’s a reason why: the whole of it
is elevated to transcendental when you see

00:18:21.700 --> 00:18:28.340
the actual thing that they’re describing,
a laggy, hideous, cheap faux-3D website that

00:18:28.340 --> 00:18:33.590
would probably be a camp hit if it were pitched
instead as a throwback to FMV video games

00:18:33.590 --> 00:18:34.679
from the 90s.

00:18:34.679 --> 00:18:36.150
“Want some Rye?

00:18:36.150 --> 00:18:38.100
Of course ya do!”

00:18:38.100 --> 00:18:42.200
The vast majority of these so-called metaverse
offerings are virtual spaces only insofar

00:18:42.200 --> 00:18:44.230
as they are painted to resemble a store.

00:18:44.230 --> 00:18:48.980
We already tried this 25 years ago, and discarded
it because it turns out the human brain can

00:18:48.980 --> 00:18:54.000
shop from a list of items or a grid of photos
far better than it can from an imagemap photo

00:18:54.000 --> 00:18:55.670
of a display case.

00:18:55.670 --> 00:18:59.419
Examples like this betray not only the ignorance
of history on the part of the people pushing

00:18:59.419 --> 00:19:03.220
it, but also a profound incompetence on the
part of the people building it.

00:19:03.220 --> 00:19:07.140
Rather than being a “cutting-edge virtual
shopping experience” the Lindt store is

00:19:07.140 --> 00:19:12.110
so bad, so embarrassing, that it would be
more respectable to learn that ByondXR is

00:19:12.110 --> 00:19:16.700
an outright scam, shaking down gullible corporations
afraid of being “out of touch” with the

00:19:16.700 --> 00:19:21.980
future or missing the boat on the next big
thing, rather than true believers trying their

00:19:21.980 --> 00:19:22.980
hardest.

00:19:22.980 --> 00:19:27.450
The fact that Patrick Diggelmann, vice president
of sales and e-commerce for Lindt USA, looked

00:19:27.450 --> 00:19:33.600
at this final product and didn’t immediately
scrap it, bury it, and demand the company’s

00:19:33.600 --> 00:19:38.960
money back is just a nice, tight encapsulation
of how much metaverse participation is being

00:19:38.960 --> 00:19:41.890
driven by irrational, uninformed FOMO.

00:19:41.890 --> 00:19:43.950
And this brings us back to Decentraland.&nbsp;

00:19:43.950 --> 00:19:46.850
All these definitions are pretty bad for Decentraland.

00:19:46.850 --> 00:19:51.500
It clearly wants to be the Metaverse, it calls
itself that, but because they, like Lindt,

00:19:51.500 --> 00:19:55.419
have made the mistake of actually trying to
make something, it's possible to evaluate

00:19:55.419 --> 00:19:56.960
whether or not they’ve succeeded.&nbsp;

00:19:56.960 --> 00:20:00.429
They have activated the trap card of falsifiability.

00:20:00.429 --> 00:20:06.059
VR was dropped from Decentraland’s core
feature set somewhere in 2018 or 2019, and

00:20:06.059 --> 00:20:11.000
has all but officially been relegated to a
community produced mod, which just means that

00:20:11.000 --> 00:20:16.299
it’s difficult to get working in the first
place, and when you do nothing is optimized

00:20:16.299 --> 00:20:20.610
for VR, either in terms of technical performance
or user experience.

00:20:20.610 --> 00:20:24.540
The results are dizzying even without a headset
on.

00:20:24.540 --> 00:20:33.660
Hnnng, okay so the app’s just straight up
broken now.

00:20:33.660 --> 00:20:37.330
Cool.

00:20:37.330 --> 00:20:43.020
For many definitions of the metaverse, like
Ball, this already makes DCL a complete non-starter:

00:20:43.020 --> 00:20:47.970
virtual reality, or at the very least augmented
reality, is a non-negotiable feature.

00:20:47.970 --> 00:20:53.600
This, however, in my evaluation is just a
case of No True Metaverse.&nbsp;

00:20:53.600 --> 00:20:57.950
Decentraland is embarrassing, which makes
it inconvenient, which means there’s a lot

00:20:57.950 --> 00:21:04.380
of incentive to discredit it as a claim to
the idea of the metaverse, to insist that

00:21:04.380 --> 00:21:08.950
its failures are meaningless, that there’s
nothing to learn from them about the viability

00:21:08.950 --> 00:21:11.230
of the underlying ideas.

00:21:11.230 --> 00:21:16.429
But the knife that says all these definitions
are the metaverse cuts both ways.

00:21:16.429 --> 00:21:21.611
If the concept is so broad as to be little
more than a vague gesture at the future, if

00:21:21.611 --> 00:21:28.580
successful and popular things like Minecraft
and Roblox and Fortnite get to be the nascent

00:21:28.580 --> 00:21:30.840
metaverse, then this does too.

00:21:30.840 --> 00:21:44.850
And if this is the future, then the future
is a dead mall.

00:21:44.850 --> 00:21:49.580
It is difficult to fully encapsulate the sheer
contradiction between Decentraland as it exists

00:21:49.580 --> 00:21:53.900
in the minds of its supporters and the actual
product that you can interact with, but in

00:21:53.900 --> 00:21:56.620
this endeavor we shall strive.

00:21:56.620 --> 00:22:01.169
Maybe we spent our best shot early by opening
with Redditors praising a theater with non-functional

00:22:01.169 --> 00:22:06.279
doors and a photo of a real concession stand
in place of any actual modeling, but it’s

00:22:06.279 --> 00:22:09.360
probably best to let you know what you’re
in for.

00:22:09.360 --> 00:22:13.470
That post that we ran earlier, by the by,
is the most popular post of all time on the

00:22:13.470 --> 00:22:15.030
Decentraland subreddit.

00:22:15.030 --> 00:22:18.500
The fact that it’s a crossover event with
Reddit’s memestock fanatics, right down

00:22:18.500 --> 00:22:23.620
to being disproportionately laden with Reddit
awards as they tend to do, has a poetry to

00:22:23.620 --> 00:22:25.450
it that I couldn’t script.&nbsp;

00:22:25.450 --> 00:22:32.360
Name a more iconic duo than bad products and
serial bagholders: you can’t do it.

00:22:32.360 --> 00:22:36.840
The pizza kiosk they rave about, where you
could, in theory, order Domino’s via the

00:22:36.840 --> 00:22:41.809
metaverse and pay in ETH or MANA is long gone,
all that remains is an empty plot devoid of

00:22:41.809 --> 00:22:44.809
even the world’s procedurally generated
shrubbery.&nbsp;

00:22:44.809 --> 00:22:49.120
The details are thin, but from what’s available
it’s apparent that Domino’s wasn’t officially

00:22:49.120 --> 00:22:53.990
involved in any capacity, which would indicate
that the builder had really just created a

00:22:53.990 --> 00:22:58.960
middleman between you and Domino’s, where
he would take your crypto and then pass the

00:22:58.960 --> 00:23:03.429
order, along with payment in dollars, to Domino’s
on your behalf.

00:23:03.429 --> 00:23:07.930
The event calls back to one of the foundational
myths of crypto, the 10,000 bitcoin pizza,

00:23:07.930 --> 00:23:12.520
the first purchase ever made with cryptocurrency.

00:23:12.520 --> 00:23:21.200
“22nd of May, 2010 is the date when someone
first purchased an item with bitcoin, in this

00:23:21.200 --> 00:23:22.539
case a pizza.”

00:23:22.539 --> 00:23:27.809
The reality of that is very similar to the
Decentraland pizza kiosk: no vendor accepted

00:23:27.809 --> 00:23:29.799
bitcoin in exchange for pizza.&nbsp;

00:23:29.799 --> 00:23:34.630
Floridian Laszlo Hanyecz offered 10,000 bitcoin
on a forum to anyone who would bring him two

00:23:34.630 --> 00:23:35.710
large pizzas.

00:23:35.710 --> 00:23:40.270
Fellow Floridian Jeremy Sturdivant took him
up on the offer, bought two large pizzas from

00:23:40.270 --> 00:23:45.520
a pizzeria with his own money and then traded
them to Laszlo in exchange for the bitcoin.

00:23:45.520 --> 00:23:49.480
Hey, that’s backwards.&nbsp;

00:23:49.480 --> 00:23:54.220
Wait, it’s also backwards from this side!

00:23:54.220 --> 00:23:58.140
Other groups have tried to recreate amusement
rides in Decentraland, such as those found

00:23:58.140 --> 00:23:59.970
in District X.&nbsp;

00:23:59.970 --> 00:24:03.660
Originally slated to be Decentraland’s Red
Light District before the project organizer

00:24:03.660 --> 00:24:08.400
stole all the donated LAND, District X is
now home to an excellent showcase of the reality

00:24:08.400 --> 00:24:14.630
of Decentraland’s ability to deliver interactive
experiences: laggy, glitchy, and profoundly

00:24:14.630 --> 00:24:15.630
sad.

00:24:15.630 --> 00:24:20.570
Oh yes, I can feel the thrills coursing through
my veins.

00:24:20.570 --> 00:24:25.120
In the Decentraland experience there’s a
persistent tension between the different vectors

00:24:25.120 --> 00:24:26.860
of incompetence.&nbsp;

00:24:26.860 --> 00:24:30.860
Most of the people building this stuff don’t
have good ideas, so the stuff is generally

00:24:30.860 --> 00:24:36.010
bad from first principles, but then on top
of that the tools for building things in Decentraland

00:24:36.010 --> 00:24:41.430
are also bad, so good ideas are kneecapped
by being extremely hard to implement,&nbsp;

00:24:41.430 --> 00:24:45.950
but then on top of that Decentraland just
runs and performs poorly, the controls are

00:24:45.950 --> 00:24:50.649
particularly heinous and sometimes the camera
gets possessed by a thousand angry ghosts,

00:24:50.649 --> 00:24:57.670
so no matter what experience you build it’ll
be tainted by that mandatory membrane of interaction.

00:24:57.670 --> 00:25:02.020
Some of what you’ll stumble across is almost
quaint, like this modern style suburban single

00:25:02.020 --> 00:25:05.520
family home populated entirely by official
Nintendo art.&nbsp;

00:25:05.520 --> 00:25:10.610
It’s almost adorably incompetent, like the
Decentraland version of a Geocities shrine

00:25:10.610 --> 00:25:12.990
to Xander from Buffy the Vampire Slayer.

00:25:12.990 --> 00:25:16.830
At least until you notice the For Sale sign
out front, and the several dozen identical

00:25:16.830 --> 00:25:20.980
Mario houses scattered around the world, all
owned by the same guy named Dan.&nbsp;

00:25:20.980 --> 00:25:26.320
They’re everywhere, I’ve completely lost
track of how many unique instances of this

00:25:26.320 --> 00:25:28.299
house I’ve stumbled across.&nbsp;

00:25:28.299 --> 00:25:32.700
This is in part because navigating Decentraland
is a nightmare, the extremely short draw distance

00:25:32.700 --> 00:25:36.809
and attendant lack of landmarks makes it very
easy to get lost, so it’s hard to tell if

00:25:36.809 --> 00:25:41.110
any given Modern Mario House is a fresh one
you’ve never been to before, or one that

00:25:41.110 --> 00:25:42.640
you’ve looped back on.

00:25:42.640 --> 00:25:45.790
 “AUG 1, 2022

00:25:45.790 --> 00:25:50.140
DecentRally: Setting the Track for Future
Experience Creators Using Infinity Engine

00:25:50.140 --> 00:25:51.140
Code

00:25:51.140 --> 00:25:55.559
The first of its kind in Decentraland, DecentRally
shows what the Infinity Engine code can do,

00:25:55.559 --> 00:25:59.740
serving as an open source foundation for future
high-speed experiences.”

00:25:59.740 --> 00:26:03.470
Decentraland faces a fascinating problem inherent
to its basic construction.

00:26:03.470 --> 00:26:08.460
The language of interaction with the world
of Decentraland is the language of games.

00:26:08.460 --> 00:26:13.370
You navigate the world via an avatar, your
avatar runs, jumps, and clicks on things.

00:26:13.370 --> 00:26:18.980
This makes games the most obvious implementation
as an Experience, since the ludology is already

00:26:18.980 --> 00:26:20.110
there.

00:26:20.110 --> 00:26:26.120
The problem is that Decentraland is just bad
for games.

00:26:26.120 --> 00:26:30.309
DecentRally is an abysmal little experience,
barely qualifying as the kart racer that it

00:26:30.309 --> 00:26:31.840
nominally resembles.

00:26:31.840 --> 00:26:35.789
Of course we can’t judge it too harshly
as it’s just a proof of concept, though

00:26:35.789 --> 00:26:39.309
given that everything in Decentraland seems
to fall under that umbrella it’s enough

00:26:39.309 --> 00:26:43.950
to make one suspicious, like maybe “it’s
still in beta” or “it’s just a proof

00:26:43.950 --> 00:26:47.570
of concept” are just a rhetorical shield
against criticism.

00:26:47.570 --> 00:26:52.250
Either which way, the developer’s other
project, Infinity Engine, is also awful, and

00:26:52.250 --> 00:26:56.390
it’s been featured on Decentraland’s front
page for well over a year.

00:26:56.390 --> 00:27:01.030
The fantasy of the metaverse is one where
it subsumes much of our present reality, where,

00:27:01.030 --> 00:27:06.820
in the words of Matthew Ball “entire generations
will eventually move to and live inside it”

00:27:06.820 --> 00:27:12.130
which, as a statement, more or less demands
poetic interpretation, that Ball is referring

00:27:12.130 --> 00:27:17.190
to interactions and activity taking place
via the membrane of the software, because

00:27:17.190 --> 00:27:22.710
otherwise it’s, you know, stupid, because
your body won’t fit inside the wires.

00:27:22.710 --> 00:27:28.760
But regardless, this idea of place requires
the simulacrum of place, and the simulacrum

00:27:28.760 --> 00:27:34.750
of place must encompass both the fantastic
and the mundane, because if it doesn’t encompass

00:27:34.750 --> 00:27:40.080
both Skyrim and your office and the grocery
store then there’s too much discontinuity,

00:27:40.080 --> 00:27:45.880
the metaverse ceases to be a world, a true
place, and becomes little more than a digital

00:27:45.880 --> 00:27:52.110
interface, and to that end the existence of
the metaverse demands the existence of Eashoo

00:27:52.110 --> 00:27:55.530
Law (A Professional Corporation).

00:27:55.530 --> 00:27:59.370
Welcome to the metaverse law office of James
Eashoo, a personal injury lawyer based in

00:27:59.370 --> 00:28:00.370
Los Angeles.

00:28:00.370 --> 00:28:04.580
James isn’t in right now, no one is, and
he never will be because the idea of staffing

00:28:04.580 --> 00:28:07.430
this place with actual people is patently
absurd.

00:28:07.430 --> 00:28:12.740
While it was unfortunate that you tripped
and dropped the urn that contained you grandmother’s

00:28:12.740 --> 00:28:19.030
ashes because you tripped over a rake that
your roommate left out, unfortunately when

00:28:19.030 --> 00:28:28.710
you attacked him with that rake it was a,
uhm, a crime, and so I’m afraid I’m going

00:28:28.710 --> 00:28:31.620
to have to refer you to a criminal defense
lawyer.

00:28:31.620 --> 00:28:41.000
Uh, this is, this is outside kinda the purview
of what I, what I do, I’m more of a personal

00:28:41.000 --> 00:28:42.020
injury lawyer.

00:28:42.020 --> 00:28:51.940
So, um, unfortunately you are going to need
criminal defense representation and, um, I

00:28:51.940 --> 00:28:55.950
do have some people tha tI can refer you to.

00:28:55.950 --> 00:29:06.620
I’m deeply sorry for the loss of your grandmother
and the death of your roommate, but I can’t

00:29:06.620 --> 00:29:11.309
assist you any further in this matter.

00:29:11.309 --> 00:29:14.480
This is not meaningfully useful in any way
to businesses.

00:29:14.480 --> 00:29:18.430
When confronted with the actual product, the
actual series of things that would need to

00:29:18.430 --> 00:29:22.660
be engaged with to make the fictional vision
of the metaverse real, the product, and the

00:29:22.660 --> 00:29:26.980
comparison to the “next generation of the
internet” is comical.

00:29:26.980 --> 00:29:31.880
Ball quotes Tim Sweeny talking about a future
where every company is expected to operate

00:29:31.880 --> 00:29:36.970
a virtual world “just as every company a
few decades ago created a webpage, and then

00:29:36.970 --> 00:29:40.279
at some point every company created a Facebook
page.”

00:29:40.279 --> 00:29:45.770
But here’s the thing: we already know from
those examples that most companies don’t

00:29:45.770 --> 00:29:51.080
need much more to a website than some cursory
fluff and their contact information.

00:29:51.080 --> 00:29:57.120
A website, to most companies, is just a replacement
for a yellow pages entry, it’s an ad whose

00:29:57.120 --> 00:30:01.690
number one job is to carry a phone number,
address, and business hours.

00:30:01.690 --> 00:30:06.309
A Facebook page became appealing because it
offloaded the work of designing the page and

00:30:06.309 --> 00:30:13.040
its functionality as a whole in a way that
office workers could easily manage: Facebook

00:30:13.040 --> 00:30:18.230
handles running Facebook, you just put in
your stuff, making it a useful tool for high

00:30:18.230 --> 00:30:23.760
density, up to date communication, like promotions
or changes in operating hours.

00:30:23.760 --> 00:30:27.899
The entire process there is a move towards
efficiency.

00:30:27.899 --> 00:30:32.190
The construction and maintenance of a virtual
world is ludicrous in comparison.

00:30:32.190 --> 00:30:36.660
The effort to build and update is orders of
magnitude beyond even a basic webpage, and

00:30:36.660 --> 00:30:39.149
the information density goes down!

00:30:39.149 --> 00:30:44.360
Like, this is really important: most business
communication is going to be in the form of

00:30:44.360 --> 00:30:51.080
text for… ever, because text is really,
really, really useful.

00:30:51.080 --> 00:30:56.500
But the metaverse is a uniquely terrible medium
for the delivery of text because that text

00:30:56.500 --> 00:31:02.850
cannot be presented in an optimized format,
it must be bent to the metaphor of a real

00:31:02.850 --> 00:31:03.850
space.

00:31:03.850 --> 00:31:08.570
That’s why the only actual meaningful things
in Eashoo Law (A Professional Corporation)

00:31:08.570 --> 00:31:12.730
are links to conventional, flat, text-and-image
based websites.

00:31:12.730 --> 00:31:17.779
Because they’re just more efficient ways
to present and access information.

00:31:17.779 --> 00:31:24.559
So the virtual workspace, the idea that this
plot in Decentraland would ever become actually

00:31:24.559 --> 00:31:30.850
functional as a space, with the receptionist
navigating up the virtual stairs to tell James,

00:31:30.850 --> 00:31:35.659
via in-world voice chat, that someone is waiting
to see him, is comical.

00:31:35.659 --> 00:31:40.490
But without that promise, without that story,
what’s even left?&nbsp;

00:31:40.490 --> 00:31:45.570
An NFT gallery and hard-to-use widgets that
just open Instagram in another tab and remind

00:31:45.570 --> 00:31:50.039
you that you’re not “in the metaverse”
you’re just browsing a website?

00:31:50.039 --> 00:31:55.540
I guess at least you can stand on the balcony
and admire the Modern Mario House across the

00:31:55.540 --> 00:31:56.540
street.

00:31:56.540 --> 00:32:01.200
Even official productions, stuff created by
the developers themselves or people they’ve

00:32:01.200 --> 00:32:05.160
sub-contracted, trend heavily towards embarrassing.

00:32:05.160 --> 00:32:10.240
The festival stage for the first annual Metaverse
Music Festival featuring DJ Paris Hilton lacked

00:32:10.240 --> 00:32:14.860
collision on the stairs and balcony, meaning
you could take a terrible elevator to a second

00:32:14.860 --> 00:32:17.309
floor that you would immediately fall through.

00:32:17.309 --> 00:32:22.669
New Years Eve 2022 was staged in a Duke-Nukem-esque
recreation of Times Square and featured a

00:32:22.669 --> 00:32:28.720
devastatingly terrible jumping puzzle, just
indescribably bad in its fundamental construction,

00:32:28.720 --> 00:32:33.240
riddled with all manner of collision problems,
and very much coming off as a half-hearted

00:32:33.240 --> 00:32:39.419
idea that was implemented improvisationally
and never actually tested, let alone revised,

00:32:39.419 --> 00:32:43.919
with the reward being a broken scavenger token,
z-fighting textures, and a bad view of the

00:32:43.919 --> 00:32:45.500
party.

00:32:45.500 --> 00:32:50.240
[barely audible fireworks]

00:32:50.240 --> 00:32:52.550
“Wait, is that it?

00:32:52.550 --> 00:32:56.250
Was that it?”

00:32:56.250 --> 00:32:59.530
After a long day’s virtual lawyering, it’s
only natural that you’ll want to take a

00:32:59.530 --> 00:33:00.530
load off.

00:33:00.530 --> 00:33:05.070
And we would recommend none-other than NFT
World’s NFT World City’s Comedy Club.&nbsp;

00:33:05.070 --> 00:33:09.779
It’s always bugged me when I go into a comedy
club and I’m not immediately met with comedy

00:33:09.779 --> 00:33:11.770
- it’s false advertising.

00:33:11.770 --> 00:33:17.210
But with the power of the metaverse, and a
$120,000 grant from the Decentraland DAO,

00:33:17.210 --> 00:33:21.309
the NFT World City’s Comedy Club obtained
an infinite source of comedy.

00:33:21.309 --> 00:33:25.830
“Uh, we hope to have content from Decentral
Comedy soon.

00:33:25.830 --> 00:33:34.570
And we have also sourced content from an AI
tool called ChatGPT which provides some jokes

00:33:34.570 --> 00:33:36.480
for us to show here.

00:33:36.480 --> 00:33:41.970
It’s not all comedy and incompetence and
insincerity, though.

00:33:41.970 --> 00:33:45.450
Occasionally you’ll stumble across a single
plot that’s just a simple shrine to a loved

00:33:45.450 --> 00:33:49.120
one, taken away from this world, too young
and, oh, hey, it’s another Modern Mario

00:33:49.120 --> 00:33:50.120
House.

00:33:50.120 --> 00:33:54.160
One of the most consistently popular locations
in Decentraland, popular enough to warrant

00:33:54.160 --> 00:33:58.900
a functionally permanent spot on the Genesis
Plaza hot locations board, is the WonderMine

00:33:58.900 --> 00:34:01.169
meteor mining and crafting game.

00:34:01.169 --> 00:34:06.000
Now, something to stress here is that this
is, within the context of Decentraland, a

00:34:06.000 --> 00:34:07.480
success story.

00:34:07.480 --> 00:34:13.280
There are people who interface with Decentraland
solely as the medium by which they access

00:34:13.280 --> 00:34:14.669
WonderMine.&nbsp;

00:34:14.669 --> 00:34:18.179
Even just looking at the map it is one of
the few consistently occupied places where

00:34:18.179 --> 00:34:21.350
you can find other players in any reasonable
number.

00:34:21.350 --> 00:34:26.000
And it is an idle clicking game that is so
sluggish in its pace that it barely qualifies

00:34:26.000 --> 00:34:27.890
as a skinner box.

00:34:27.890 --> 00:34:30.540
Meteors fall, and you click on them.

00:34:30.540 --> 00:34:32.070
And then you wait.

00:34:32.070 --> 00:34:36.359
The sole value prospect is that at the end
of the process you might be able to craft

00:34:36.359 --> 00:34:39.560
an NFT wearable that you can then sell to
someone else.&nbsp;

00:34:39.560 --> 00:34:43.889
“So like I was saying, if you want to see
what this is worth, we can go over to the

00:34:43.889 --> 00:34:48.720
marketplace and search steampunk hat and as
you can see its worth 2499 MANA.

00:34:48.720 --> 00:34:56.560
And if we want to view the value of MANA today,
it’s over a dollar.”

00:34:56.560 --> 00:35:01.220
So with such big money on the line, of course
the game is botted to hell and back with idlers

00:35:01.220 --> 00:35:03.210
and auto-clickers.

00:35:03.210 --> 00:35:07.109
Decentraland doesn’t log users out after
a set time spent idle, and the meteors fall

00:35:07.109 --> 00:35:13.140
in predictable locations, so it’s pretty
easy to automate, but even barring that level

00:35:13.140 --> 00:35:20.050
of complexity it’s trivial to just leave
a tab idle and collect the periodic rewards.

00:35:20.050 --> 00:35:23.859
Wondermine is also immediately adjacent to
one of the oldest games in Decentraland, Dragon

00:35:23.859 --> 00:35:27.550
Rush, which is featured prominently in Genesis
Plaza.&nbsp;

00:35:27.550 --> 00:35:32.450
Immediately we can spot a problem with the
real estate model of the internet: this relatively

00:35:32.450 --> 00:35:37.320
modest game has an absolutely massive footprint
and necessitates a towering retaining wall

00:35:37.320 --> 00:35:38.829
to back its terrain.&nbsp;

00:35:38.829 --> 00:35:44.810
From a standpoint of architecture it is an
immensely hostile block, a massive impassable

00:35:44.810 --> 00:35:47.790
barrier to both sightlines and traffic.

00:35:47.790 --> 00:35:53.410
It turns out that having every individual
app and website take up scarce space in an

00:35:53.410 --> 00:35:57.869
adversarial relationship is, uh, bad.

00:35:57.869 --> 00:36:04.430
Alright, so, walk onto the dragon’s back,
press f to flap wings and fly, go through

00:36:04.430 --> 00:36:23.579
the gates and land on the landing pad to finish
the race.

00:36:23.579 --> 00:36:44.820
Now, maybe this is just unfair, after all
I did say that Dragon Rush is one of the oldest

00:36:44.820 --> 00:36:46.400
games in Decentraland.

00:36:46.400 --> 00:36:52.440
Let’s do some due diligence and find something
more mature, more refined, something with

00:36:52.440 --> 00:36:56.800
a budget behind it.

00:36:56.800 --> 00:37:04.830
Knights of Antrom, the ultimate role-playing
game that will challenge you to farm, craft,

00:37:04.830 --> 00:37:06.700
and fight your way to the top.

00:37:06.700 --> 00:37:10.210
Those looking for a little more action to
their Decentraland experience, need look no

00:37:10.210 --> 00:37:12.130
further than Dice Masters.

00:37:12.130 --> 00:37:16.180
An incredible, fully-playable role-playing
game built within the metaverse’s trusty

00:37:16.180 --> 00:37:17.190
framework.

00:37:17.190 --> 00:37:22.170
Within its sizable location, gamers can undertake
quests, battle enemies, collect resources

00:37:22.170 --> 00:37:26.359
and craft in game items, all in addition to
ridding the realm of the terrible poultry

00:37:26.359 --> 00:37:27.359
scourge.

00:37:27.359 --> 00:37:29.119
Dice Masters Development and Expansion

00:37:29.119 --> 00:37:35.521
Should the following Tier 6: up to $240,000
USD, 6 months vesting (1 month cliff) grant

00:37:35.521 --> 00:37:37.250
in the Gaming category be approved?

00:37:37.250 --> 00:37:38.890
Yes wins with 93%

00:37:38.890 --> 00:37:41.880
Okay, that’s quite a pitch.

00:37:41.880 --> 00:37:47.450
And, you know what, let’s treat this seriously,
like let’s treat this like it were a Kickstarter

00:37:47.450 --> 00:37:50.339
or Steam Early Access pitch.&nbsp;

00:37:50.339 --> 00:37:56.329
$240,000 is modest but realistic, and watching
the trailer, it’s all very achievable, especially

00:37:56.329 --> 00:37:59.170
since the buildings are just lifted from World
of Warcraft.

00:37:59.170 --> 00:38:04.869
But, like, the features are very straightforward,
and the trailer even evokes Decentraland’s

00:38:04.869 --> 00:38:05.869
style.&nbsp;

00:38:05.869 --> 00:38:07.920
This is eminently realizable.

00:38:07.920 --> 00:38:08.920
Low rent?

00:38:08.920 --> 00:38:09.920
Sure.

00:38:09.920 --> 00:38:11.349
Devoid of original ideas?

00:38:11.349 --> 00:38:12.619
Also true.

00:38:12.619 --> 00:38:13.619
Doable?

00:38:13.619 --> 00:38:14.619
Absolutely.

00:38:14.619 --> 00:38:15.619
This is it, right?

00:38:15.619 --> 00:38:20.140
This is the moment Decentraland has been waiting
for, this is the realization of all their

00:38:20.140 --> 00:38:21.140
promises!

00:38:21.140 --> 00:38:26.579
This isn’t just a thing in Decentraland,
it’s Decentraland as a platform, as the

00:38:26.579 --> 00:38:31.670
infrastructure invisible beneath an otherwise
independent game experience!

00:38:31.670 --> 00:38:36.329
They even bothered to include the coordinates
in the trailer, like they actually want you

00:38:36.329 --> 00:38:59.780
to go there!

00:38:59.780 --> 00:39:07.590
[groaning]

00:39:07.590 --> 00:39:34.329
It’s just… it’s the same story over
and over again, everywhere you go in Decentraland.

00:39:34.329 --> 00:39:36.560
It’s all the same stuff.

00:39:36.560 --> 00:39:41.880
No amount of remixing can overcome the underlying
flaws of the base layer.

00:39:41.880 --> 00:39:47.530
Decentraland wants to be the invisible tech
behind the scenes, but it can’t because

00:39:47.530 --> 00:39:53.670
it also wants to be a real virtual world,
and the one goal imposes endless creative

00:39:53.670 --> 00:39:56.200
and technical decisions onto the other.

00:39:56.200 --> 00:40:12.390
You can’t really get anything better out
of the system!

00:40:12.390 --> 00:40:17.490
The MetaGamiMall is a construction of special
note because its existence was made possible

00:40:17.490 --> 00:40:24.840
by a grant of $220,000 US dollars from the
Decentraland DAO, the organization that operates,

00:40:24.840 --> 00:40:29.060
in theory, as the government of the Decentraland
world.

00:40:29.060 --> 00:40:33.619
The pitch was a gamified mall, and I will
let their own pitch package speak for itself

00:40:33.619 --> 00:40:34.820
on some key details.

00:40:34.820 --> 00:40:37.170
“Game + Mall = GamiMall”

00:40:37.170 --> 00:40:43.940
“The game - super dogerio, the vitality
of the mall.&nbsp;

00:40:43.940 --> 00:40:49.839
“The P2E economic model can incentivise
users to play and earn.&nbsp;

00:40:49.839 --> 00:40:54.020
“The first comprehensive traffic road system
in Decentaland.

00:40:54.020 --> 00:40:57.339
Highly compatible with Customer shopping habits
and Super Dogerio Game.

00:40:57.339 --> 00:40:58.800
Interesting piano floor, create more fun while
walking and running.”

00:40:58.800 --> 00:41:03.430
“The Meta GamiMall is an avant gard project
endeavor to blur the boundaries between game

00:41:03.430 --> 00:41:06.319
and shop activities.

00:41:06.319 --> 00:41:16.940
The game lives in the mall, the mall needs
the game.”

00:41:16.940 --> 00:41:21.170
The actual product is a track that you walk
along to collect coins that can be traded

00:41:21.170 --> 00:41:25.890
for wearables, while a bad techno DJ repeats
the instructions on an endless loop.&nbsp;

00:41:25.890 --> 00:41:28.619
&nbsp;
Because space is so tight, even for large

00:41:28.619 --> 00:41:33.250
plots like this, the track consists mostly
of really obnoxious spirals, and the place

00:41:33.250 --> 00:41:37.650
is infested with bots that idle on the exact
same spot until the timer resets and they

00:41:37.650 --> 00:41:39.480
can collect more coins.&nbsp;

00:41:39.480 --> 00:41:44.450
It would seem that while $220,000 can get
you a lot of stuff, it can’t get you an

00:41:44.450 --> 00:41:47.840
experience worth doing with your own human
hands.&nbsp;

00:41:47.840 --> 00:41:53.250
The actual rules of Super Dogerio are oblique,
and the DJ’s instructions don’t actually

00:41:53.250 --> 00:41:54.250
help.

00:41:54.250 --> 00:41:56.670
“Welcome to MetaMine.

00:41:56.670 --> 00:42:02.599
MetaMine is a metaverse game event that will
begin from 18th Novermber at MetaGamiMall

00:42:02.599 --> 00:42:03.599
in Decentraland.”

00:42:03.599 --> 00:42:07.230
“MetaMine is a metaverse game event”

00:42:07.230 --> 00:42:10.530
I guess it’s called Metamine now?

00:42:10.530 --> 00:42:13.349
As for the mall component, I don’t even
know what to say.&nbsp;

00:42:13.349 --> 00:42:17.480
The space is so cluttered with the extreme
cyberspace aesthetic that it’s barely even

00:42:17.480 --> 00:42:22.240
legible as a mall, and the whole notion is
keelhauled by the fact that this is a miserable

00:42:22.240 --> 00:42:27.160
interface for actually buying things, which
is the actual purpose of a mall.&nbsp;

00:42:27.160 --> 00:42:31.860
The result, an empty shopping experience plagued
with bots, isn’t just a dead mall, it’s

00:42:31.860 --> 00:42:35.250
an undead mall, stuttering along in a parody
of life.

00:42:35.250 --> 00:42:42.099
Now, they did seek a new grant in late 2022,
asking for an additional $60,000 USD to expand

00:42:42.099 --> 00:42:44.250
and revitalize the mall.

00:42:44.250 --> 00:42:48.800
Unfortunately the proposal was rejected 3
million to 9 million, with 7.8 million of

00:42:48.800 --> 00:42:51.450
the “no” votes coming from two people.

00:42:51.450 --> 00:42:54.619
Sorry MetaGamiMall, better luck next time.

00:42:54.619 --> 00:42:58.480
The Decentraland Report is one of the most
fascinating elements of Decentraland, and

00:42:58.480 --> 00:43:02.329
it breaks my heart that we can’t give it
the time it truly deserves.

00:43:02.329 --> 00:43:06.620
They style themselves as “the first ever
decentralized news network, reporting on the

00:43:06.620 --> 00:43:09.380
latest breaking news of the Metaverse”.

00:43:09.380 --> 00:43:14.599
Between grants and private funding it has
received over $250,000 USD, a quarter million

00:43:14.599 --> 00:43:16.119
dollars of investment.

00:43:16.119 --> 00:43:21.069
It is an organization operated by Kevin Clark,
better known by his handle KevinOnEarth999.

00:43:21.069 --> 00:43:24.230
We’ll let him explain what it means:

00:43:24.230 --> 00:43:29.760
“Decentralized news networks give the public
the power to control event coverage, reporters

00:43:29.760 --> 00:43:33.119
and how the network will evolve over time.

00:43:33.119 --> 00:43:37.319
Tokens are distributed to the public based
on participation and can be used to purchase

00:43:37.319 --> 00:43:42.730
event coverage, ad space, and vote on key
decisions - such as the addition or removal

00:43:42.730 --> 00:43:44.780
of actual news reporters.”

00:43:44.780 --> 00:43:48.680
Just to stress this, to highlight it and make
sure that you are aware that this is not us

00:43:48.680 --> 00:43:55.819
catastrophizing or editorializing, the ability
for the crowd to fire reporters is an explicit

00:43:55.819 --> 00:43:56.819
feature.

00:43:56.819 --> 00:44:00.490
“Imagine if you could use a token to vote
off a reporter from the news.”

00:44:00.490 --> 00:44:04.460
You can buy the ability to fire a reporter.

00:44:04.460 --> 00:44:08.900
And that’s deliberate, because it gives
the token resale value, which incentivises

00:44:08.900 --> 00:44:09.900
participation.

00:44:09.900 --> 00:44:15.280
The editorial agenda of the Decentraland Report
is not just nominally available for purchase,

00:44:15.280 --> 00:44:18.579
it is proudly available for purchase.

00:44:18.579 --> 00:44:22.180
The Decentraland Report is a news outlet that
hates journalism.

00:44:22.180 --> 00:44:26.820
It takes the very messy subject of journalistic
independence, the way that journalism often

00:44:26.820 --> 00:44:32.530
functions as willing collaborators with systemic
power, and the reasons why we should be legitimately

00:44:32.530 --> 00:44:38.540
skeptical, alert, and literate in how we intake
media, and uses that to advocate for a radical

00:44:38.540 --> 00:44:41.650
populism masquerading as neutrality.

00:44:41.650 --> 00:44:47.859
The logic is that institutions are inherently
biased, therefore less institution means less

00:44:47.859 --> 00:44:51.990
bias, and no institution means no bias.

00:44:51.990 --> 00:44:56.170
Their trust model is explicitly rooted in
their incompetence.

00:44:56.170 --> 00:45:00.630
Kevin’s rhetoric is part and parcel with
the conspiratorial thinking that leads people

00:45:00.630 --> 00:45:06.320
to trust misinformation they found on Facebook
specifically because they found it on a page

00:45:06.320 --> 00:45:10.240
that gets flagged by Facebook as misinformation.

00:45:10.240 --> 00:45:16.010
It shares a table with the belief that reporters
exist to deceive the public, and so the only

00:45:16.010 --> 00:45:20.010
journalism you can trust comes from those
who hate journalism.

00:45:20.010 --> 00:45:25.569
“With this decentralized approach to news,
the public can take control of global narratives

00:45:25.569 --> 00:45:31.280
and focus on fact-based reporting verses biased-based
reporting which is focused more on getting

00:45:31.280 --> 00:45:33.859
views than telling the truth.

00:45:33.859 --> 00:45:38.450
Kevin’s vision for the news is downright
dystopian, it’s a killer writing prompt

00:45:38.450 --> 00:45:40.250
if you’re in need of ideas.

00:45:40.250 --> 00:45:42.070
So what’s the product?

00:45:42.070 --> 00:45:46.220
You want to see some journalism so unprofessional
that it would make even the writers at the

00:45:46.220 --> 00:45:47.270
Defiant cringe?

00:45:47.270 --> 00:45:48.270
“Any giveaways?

00:45:48.270 --> 00:45:49.930
Yeah, for sure man!

00:45:49.930 --> 00:45:54.260
If you hang around I’ll definitely do a
giveaway man!

00:45:54.260 --> 00:45:56.410
So we’re just hop right on in here to Dice
Masters.

00:45:56.410 --> 00:45:57.640
Come on through!

00:45:57.640 --> 00:46:02.150
I just want to show support to Dice Masters.”

00:46:02.150 --> 00:46:09.530
“And then we’ll kinda move into the auction
phase, hopping from gallery to gallery.”

00:46:09.530 --> 00:46:13.290
“That is, that sounds like a great time.”

00:46:13.290 --> 00:46:17.710
“As recommended by Seanny, upon the passing
of the grant, we agree to pay back 10% of

00:46:17.710 --> 00:46:21.990
our monthly revenue to the DAO, in perpetuity
until the grant amount is paid back to the

00:46:21.990 --> 00:46:22.990
DAO.

00:46:22.990 --> 00:46:23.990
That’s amazing.

00:46:23.990 --> 00:46:26.809
That, to me, is epic.”

00:46:26.809 --> 00:46:29.950
It’s just Twitch streams.

00:46:29.950 --> 00:46:32.650
“No fucking way!

00:46:32.650 --> 00:46:37.770
Do you fucking hear that motherfuckers!

00:46:37.770 --> 00:46:42.310
That’s what the fucking metaverse was missing!

00:46:42.310 --> 00:46:43.310
Metal!”

00:46:43.310 --> 00:46:45.160
“That is very true, holy shit.

00:46:45.160 --> 00:46:50.010
That is very frickin’ true.

00:46:50.010 --> 00:46:51.940
Wow.&nbsp;

00:46:51.940 --> 00:46:54.350
That was Big Dave.&nbsp;

00:46:54.350 --> 00:46:57.230
Says Big Dave is Talking, listen the fuck
up.

00:46:57.230 --> 00:46:58.480
&nbsp;Wow.&nbsp;

00:46:58.480 --> 00:47:02.220
That was, wow.

00:47:02.220 --> 00:47:03.460
Wow.&nbsp;

00:47:03.460 --> 00:47:04.610
That was sound check?

00:47:04.610 --> 00:47:08.059
You gotta be kidding me!

00:47:08.059 --> 00:47:11.730
That was frickin’ sound check, you gotta
be kidding me!”

00:47:11.730 --> 00:47:13.580
I know, I know, right?

00:47:13.580 --> 00:47:17.930
It’s pretty disappointing that after all
that talk about the philosophy of journalism

00:47:17.930 --> 00:47:22.840
this ship runs aground over the fact that
nothing remotely worth reporting on happens

00:47:22.840 --> 00:47:28.490
in Decentraland in the first place, so the
risk of journalistic malpractice is functionally

00:47:28.490 --> 00:47:29.590
zero.

00:47:29.590 --> 00:47:33.630
Despite the lofty ambitions of the network,
these guys are ultimately harmless.

00:47:33.630 --> 00:47:37.950
They’re just small streamers cosplaying
as reporters while hanging out with their

00:47:37.950 --> 00:47:42.190
handful of regulars talking about the video
game they like to play.

00:47:42.190 --> 00:47:44.740
That said, there’s still a story here.

00:47:44.740 --> 00:47:48.560
They still raised a quarter million dollars,
right?

00:47:48.560 --> 00:47:51.920
Did that at least pay someone?

00:47:51.920 --> 00:47:53.990
Well, no.

00:47:53.990 --> 00:47:55.859
Reporters aren’t “paid” as such.

00:47:55.859 --> 00:48:00.210
They get paid in the REPORT token, and they
get a cut of the organizations’ monthly

00:48:00.210 --> 00:48:05.059
revenue on Xpand, a crypto-meets-Twitter-meets-Patreon
platform.&nbsp;

00:48:05.059 --> 00:48:09.120
At time of writing that’s around $120 bucks,
total.

00:48:09.120 --> 00:48:13.960
Xpand takes a 10% cut, there is another 10%
cut for their grant repayment, a phrase that

00:48:13.960 --> 00:48:17.010
shouldn’t exist, and then the remainder
is split nine ways.

00:48:17.010 --> 00:48:21.520
There’s the possibility of one-time donations
on top of that, which get cut the same way,

00:48:21.520 --> 00:48:26.670
but on average the individual reporters are
looking at about ten bucks a month.

00:48:26.670 --> 00:48:32.079
This uh, isn’t a great deal, especially
since the streamers,&nbsp;

00:48:32.079 --> 00:48:33.950
Sorry, reporters

00:48:33.950 --> 00:48:36.119
Need to provide their own equipment.&nbsp;

00:48:36.119 --> 00:48:40.040
So it's no surprise that Kevin has failed
to draw in any Pulitzer Prize winners.

00:48:40.040 --> 00:48:44.200
Who he’s ended up with is an ensemble of
Decentraland users who just want to grow their

00:48:44.200 --> 00:48:46.660
stream by associating with an org.

00:48:46.660 --> 00:48:50.450
They’re all broadcasting from their personal
Twitch streams, what they get from the org

00:48:50.450 --> 00:48:55.670
is the DCLR branding, which, you know, it
doesn’t draw in the crowds.

00:48:55.670 --> 00:49:00.700
Every single stream we caught had extremely
low viewership, almost always fewer than 10

00:49:00.700 --> 00:49:02.640
viewers, often under 5.

00:49:02.640 --> 00:49:07.370
Kevin's personal and DCLR branded YouTube
channels don’t fare any better - a video

00:49:07.370 --> 00:49:10.309
is a success if it breaks 100 views.

00:49:10.309 --> 00:49:15.270
So, where on (Kevin)OnEarth did the money
go?

00:49:15.270 --> 00:49:21.460
Okay, the reporters only get paid from the
Xpand pot, DCLR doesn’t buy them lights

00:49:21.460 --> 00:49:33.200
or webcams, the only thing left to even spend
it on… is Decentraland itself?

00:49:33.200 --> 00:49:40.105
[Kazoo and fart performance of Also Sprach
Zarathustra]

00:49:40.105 --> 00:49:43.600
The Decentraland Report is the perfect showcase
of web3’s ability to cause money to just

00:49:43.600 --> 00:49:45.089
evaporate.

00:49:45.089 --> 00:49:49.880
The build is called the Metaflower, which
is a reference to an NFT of a superyacht from

00:49:49.880 --> 00:49:52.839
other metaverse candidate The Sandbox that
sold&nbsp;

00:49:52.839 --> 00:49:55.359
Know what, doesn’t matter.&nbsp;

00:49:55.359 --> 00:49:59.579
Using allegedly very expensive tech, the flower
is intended to develop and bloom as the number

00:49:59.579 --> 00:50:02.400
of subscribers on the Xpand page grows.

00:50:02.400 --> 00:50:06.839
Of course we need to take Kevin’s word on
that because the count is currently 22 followers

00:50:06.839 --> 00:50:08.950
toward the goal of 10,000.

00:50:08.950 --> 00:50:12.350
So the building has remained in its initial
form.

00:50:12.350 --> 00:50:14.130
What we’re left with is this.

00:50:14.130 --> 00:50:19.170
We’ve got walls laced with Kevin’s prized
NFTs, two platforms that I’m assured are

00:50:19.170 --> 00:50:22.100
the ‘studios’ where presenters are intended
to stand.

00:50:22.100 --> 00:50:27.550
The Decision Room, where DCLR DAO members
will meet, once the DAO gets some.

00:50:27.550 --> 00:50:31.770
And then we have the butthole tunnel that
takes us to an alien in a rice paddy hat who

00:50:31.770 --> 00:50:36.880
tells us that we need to save the Decentraland
Report by gathering News Juice.

00:50:36.880 --> 00:50:41.109
And it turns out that News Juice means ‘please
follow Kevin and pals on Twitter.’

00:50:41.109 --> 00:50:45.329
Wait, Kevin’s in here twice!

00:50:45.329 --> 00:50:48.160
Strangely there’s no news in the DCLR HQ.&nbsp;

00:50:48.160 --> 00:50:53.420
What they do give you is a link to an entirely
different 3D web application called Oncyber.

00:50:53.420 --> 00:50:57.000
So you’re sitting there, computer frying
from running Decentraland.

00:50:57.000 --> 00:51:01.420
And if you want to see some news at the metaverse
news building, Kevin wants you to open up

00:51:01.420 --> 00:51:07.310
another 3D site that loads up two dozen YouTube
videos at once and then crashes Firefox entirely

00:51:07.310 --> 00:51:09.550
- it’s absurd.

00:51:09.550 --> 00:51:14.160
Needless to say, the Decentraland build contributes
extremely little to the utility of running

00:51:14.160 --> 00:51:15.780
a news network.

00:51:15.780 --> 00:51:21.000
We don’t know precisely how much this whole
thing cost beyond about $35,000 for the LAND,

00:51:21.000 --> 00:51:26.510
but given that the $127,000 in grants are
reasonably well accounted for it would seem

00:51:26.510 --> 00:51:31.520
that, after the boring stuff like building
the website and Twitch assets, this as a whole

00:51:31.520 --> 00:51:35.829
represents Kevin’s $126,000.

00:51:35.829 --> 00:51:38.010
So let’s focus in on those grants.

00:51:38.010 --> 00:51:40.859
Kevin received 4 grants from the Decentraland
DAO.

00:51:40.859 --> 00:51:48.650
Two were to hire an editor for DCLR - both
paid the editor $50 per edit… in MANA.

00:51:48.650 --> 00:51:54.299
Then there was a $5000 grant in September
of 2021 to create unique in-game jackets for

00:51:54.299 --> 00:51:57.829
reporters as a milestone reward for 6 months
work.

00:51:57.829 --> 00:52:03.300
And then there was the big one, a $120,000
US grant in stable coins to build the backend

00:52:03.300 --> 00:52:05.859
for DCLR’s reward system.

00:52:05.859 --> 00:52:10.760
Since decentralized news is all about rewarding
participation, including viewership, you need

00:52:10.760 --> 00:52:12.650
to build that system.

00:52:12.650 --> 00:52:18.810
Pretty much the same tech that handles Twitch
drops… to service a community of like 30

00:52:18.810 --> 00:52:19.810
people max.&nbsp;

00:52:19.810 --> 00:52:24.510
The decentralized philosophy of the project
demands this type of massive, inefficient

00:52:24.510 --> 00:52:27.250
system exist as a starting point.

00:52:27.250 --> 00:52:30.460
You can’t expand into these solutions naturally.

00:52:30.460 --> 00:52:37.760
So Kevin spent $35,000 on LAND, potentially
$91,000 on the studio build, $5,000 on jackets,

00:52:37.760 --> 00:52:43.720
and $120,000 on this reward system, and then
went back to the grant program looking for

00:52:43.720 --> 00:52:49.530
another $240,000, 32 of which was allotted
to reporter equipment and included salaries

00:52:49.530 --> 00:52:54.150
for a marketing director, content manager,
graphic designer and so on.

00:52:54.150 --> 00:52:58.869
In what seems to have been the only lucid
moment in the fever dream of the grant scheme,

00:52:58.869 --> 00:52:59.869
Kevin was told…

00:52:59.869 --> 00:53:00.869
no.

00:53:00.869 --> 00:53:04.440
Two more reduced proposals have also been
rejected.

00:53:04.440 --> 00:53:06.490
You know what we haven’t talked about in
a while?

00:53:06.490 --> 00:53:07.490
Chocolate.

00:53:07.490 --> 00:53:13.460
I mean, sure, Lindt has their metaverse, but
surely chocolate giant Hershey wouldn’t

00:53:13.460 --> 00:53:14.600
be left behind.

00:53:14.600 --> 00:53:17.480
Well, if you’ve got a craving, you’re
in luck!

00:53:17.480 --> 00:53:21.870
Just a hop skip and a jump from the Sugar
Club you can experience Hersheyverse, the

00:53:21.870 --> 00:53:26.710
official Metaverse holiday home of Hershey
Chocolates (of India and the Philippines).

00:53:26.710 --> 00:53:31.360
Sure this plot may look microscopic, but boy
howdy is there a lot to do.

00:53:31.360 --> 00:53:36.340
For one you can talk to the shopkeepers inside
and they’ll… send you a link to Amazon.

00:53:36.340 --> 00:53:37.640
But there’s also gamification!

00:53:37.640 --> 00:53:38.910
There’s a quest!

00:53:38.910 --> 00:53:40.599
It’s time for a treasure hunt!

00:53:40.599 --> 00:53:44.630
Just hop up here and bounce over here, and
click on this, and ride the elevator and click

00:53:44.630 --> 00:53:48.710
on the camera and you can get your very own
official Hersheyverse wearable.

00:53:48.710 --> 00:53:53.030
An NFT on the frequently exploited Polygon
blockchain, it would appear that this hat

00:53:53.030 --> 00:53:58.609
has been claimed, generously, about 500 times.

00:53:58.609 --> 00:54:03.089
But once you claim your extremely ugly hat
you get one last quest to extend your time

00:54:03.089 --> 00:54:04.310
in the Hersheyverse.

00:54:04.310 --> 00:54:09.640
You see the treasure chest needs opening,
and the keys won’t find themselves, so you

00:54:09.640 --> 00:54:15.960
just, talk to the shopkeepers and pick the
keys up off the ground, and click the thing,

00:54:15.960 --> 00:54:19.609
and gain the ability to fly!

00:54:19.609 --> 00:54:24.589
And by fly I mean that if you double jump
an invisible platform spawns under you and

00:54:24.589 --> 00:54:29.290
lifts you up to a predetermined height, where
you can jump to one of the clouds and take

00:54:29.290 --> 00:54:35.870
control of the chocolate cannon which may
or may not work.

00:54:35.870 --> 00:54:37.720
Brands love the metaverse.

00:54:37.720 --> 00:54:41.660
Or at least that’s the story.

00:54:41.660 --> 00:54:45.330
Samsung, Nissan, Vodafone, Snapple, CitiBank,
Pedigree, Hershey, Mountain Dew, Trident,

00:54:45.330 --> 00:54:51.950
Sour Patch Kids, Atari, Miller Lite, JP Morgan,
everyone’s getting in on the metaverse!

00:54:51.950 --> 00:54:56.770
Now, what that means is, uh, extremely flexible.

00:54:56.770 --> 00:55:01.710
We’ve already dipped into Hersheyverse,
but what else is out there?

00:55:01.710 --> 00:55:06.160
It’s just… it’s weird, man.

00:55:06.160 --> 00:55:10.619
Atari has probably the best looking location
of any of these, but it still principally

00:55:10.619 --> 00:55:15.740
consists of a large faux-social space full
of furniture that you can’t interact with.

00:55:15.740 --> 00:55:17.830
Wait, have I even mentioned that yet?!

00:55:17.830 --> 00:55:21.380
Yeah, you can’t sit on chairs in Decentraland!&nbsp;

00:55:21.380 --> 00:55:26.570
The whole goddamn world is built of nothing
but hangout spots and you can’t even role

00:55:26.570 --> 00:55:34.480
play sitting around a table unless you buy
the user-created Sit emote from the marketplace!

00:55:34.480 --> 00:55:38.390
Otherwise in the atari stronghold you have
a slate of links that all take you out of

00:55:38.390 --> 00:55:42.559
Decentraland and a couple wearables that are
no longer available.

00:55:42.559 --> 00:55:47.309
The only thing to actually do here is play
one of the four variants of Breakout and feel

00:55:47.309 --> 00:55:54.559
the gnawing cold reality seep into you as
you realize that Decentraland is such a monumental

00:55:54.559 --> 00:56:01.500
failure as a platform for socialization, for
commerce, and for gaming that it can’t even

00:56:01.500 --> 00:56:09.160
handle properly emulating Breakout, a game
from 1976 that you can play on goddamn Google

00:56:09.160 --> 00:56:10.160
images!

00:56:10.160 --> 00:56:17.000
Steve Wozniak built Breakout fifty years ago
to run on 44 TTL chips and a ham sandwich

00:56:17.000 --> 00:56:24.520
and that’s still somehow too demanding a
gaming experience for Decentraland.

00:56:24.520 --> 00:56:29.990
The Onyx Lounge by JP Morgan Chase, built
in March 2022 and described by Fortune as

00:56:29.990 --> 00:56:34.809
a bet on “a metaverse future that could
be worth trillions” was originally staffed

00:56:34.809 --> 00:56:40.010
only by a wandering tiger, but has since been
replaced with a cramped reading lounge.

00:56:40.010 --> 00:56:46.809
The DCL branch of HSBC is a unique kind of
awful, with this deeply unsettling robot receptionist

00:56:46.809 --> 00:56:52.150
and stairs with no collision, with the only
problem being that it’s not clear if this

00:56:52.150 --> 00:56:57.339
is an official HSBC project or not, since
impersonation is trivial and even the official

00:56:57.339 --> 00:57:03.660
builds are glitchy and weird, because it’s
Decentraland: everything is glitchy and weird.

00:57:03.660 --> 00:57:08.160
Particularly though, as you go through a lot
of these you start to notice a few trends.

00:57:08.160 --> 00:57:11.450
First is a subject that we’ll talk about
at length later, which is that discussion

00:57:11.450 --> 00:57:14.660
of these ventures rarely feature anything
meaningful.

00:57:14.660 --> 00:57:19.360
They don’t tend to tell you how to actually
find the location, and tend to downplay the

00:57:19.360 --> 00:57:25.049
product itself, typically limiting visuals
to a wide screenshot of an exterior, if anything

00:57:25.049 --> 00:57:26.099
at all.

00:57:26.099 --> 00:57:29.010
Fortune didn’t even bother to mention the
tiger.

00:57:29.010 --> 00:57:34.510
It’s really more about the headline than
the actual space.

00:57:34.510 --> 00:57:36.290
Second is a trend of dispersion.

00:57:36.290 --> 00:57:41.000
It’s not Hershey’s that’s in the metaverse,
it’s Hershey’s India and Philippines,

00:57:41.000 --> 00:57:44.839
it’s Nissan Italy, it’s Vodafone Turkey.

00:57:44.839 --> 00:57:47.960
Third is a trend towards the temporary.

00:57:47.960 --> 00:57:52.609
While the Atari metaverse headquarters is
still standing and the Onyx Lounge by JP Morgan

00:57:52.609 --> 00:57:58.910
Chase still sort of technically exists, most
of these aren’t actually brands “moving

00:57:58.910 --> 00:58:02.039
to the metaverse”, they’re just promotional
campaigns.

00:58:02.039 --> 00:58:07.210
The Snapple Stronghold, Miller Lite Metalite
Bar, and Hersheyverse are already gone.

00:58:07.210 --> 00:58:13.210
“And look, we’ve heard there aren’t
a lot of people inside the Metaverse yet,

00:58:13.210 --> 00:58:15.710
but marketers say it's gonna be a very big
deal…”

00:58:15.710 --> 00:58:19.530
The Meataversity of Steak-umm came and went
in about two months.

00:58:19.530 --> 00:58:25.039
The Trident Vibe x Sour Patch Kids Chew The
Vibes Dance Party has been replaced by an

00:58:25.039 --> 00:58:29.530
“office” for MoZeus Worldwide, the ad
agency that built it.

00:58:29.530 --> 00:58:34.680
While a few corporations jumped in early and
actually bought LAND, that ship has seemingly

00:58:34.680 --> 00:58:40.240
already sailed, and now the impetus for this
isn’t originating with the brands but with

00:58:40.240 --> 00:58:46.200
the digital ad agencies that they hire, who
then toss in a Decentraland “activation”

00:58:46.200 --> 00:58:52.830
as part of the package, a way to recoup some
value from the LAND that the ad agency bought.

00:58:52.830 --> 00:58:57.480
Fourth is that they just overwhelmingly suck
and need to constantly direct users out of

00:58:57.480 --> 00:59:00.609
Decentraland in order to accomplish anything.

00:59:00.609 --> 00:59:05.480
The Nissan experience will let you book a
test drive in Italy if you click a link to

00:59:05.480 --> 00:59:07.170
open their existing website.

00:59:07.170 --> 00:59:10.250
The Atari store opens Atari’s actual store.

00:59:10.250 --> 00:59:14.859
The Pedigree Fosterverse lets LAND holders
create a metaverse duplicate of a real dog

00:59:14.859 --> 00:59:17.720
in a partnered shelter in need of a forever
home.

00:59:17.720 --> 00:59:22.829
The metaverse dog serves as a digital billboard
leading to the dog’s adoptapet page.

00:59:22.829 --> 00:59:27.400
Of the three dogs the platform lists at time
of writing, all three have long-since been

00:59:27.400 --> 00:59:32.400
adopted out, but their listings remain because
the incentive for landholders to participate

00:59:32.400 --> 00:59:36.930
is to get a Pedigree branded dog on their
parcel - so the metaverse dogs are doomed

00:59:36.930 --> 00:59:39.789
to beg for adoption for all eternity.

00:59:39.789 --> 00:59:41.680
So what does that all mean?

00:59:41.680 --> 00:59:46.900
Well, the obvious, but speculative, narrative
is that ad agencies that have a stake in the

00:59:46.900 --> 00:59:51.369
success of “the metaverse” are approaching
brands in smaller markets and pitching them

00:59:51.369 --> 00:59:55.670
on a vision of “the future” and dazzling
them with some cooked numbers.

00:59:55.670 --> 00:59:59.930
They point to articles in real outlets like
Fortune with titles referencing a trillion

00:59:59.930 --> 01:00:07.610
dollar future, and, like, in the face of that
can you even afford the possibility of being

01:00:07.610 --> 01:00:08.610
wrong?

01:00:08.610 --> 01:00:11.890
It’s the lamest version of Pascal’s Wager.

01:00:11.890 --> 01:00:15.869
Back on the Genesis Plaza spawn platform we’ve
got these pillars that I’ve shown but not

01:00:15.869 --> 01:00:17.560
really talked about yet.

01:00:17.560 --> 01:00:23.309
These are Decentraland’s solution to discoverability
in their metaverse, or, well, at least one

01:00:23.309 --> 01:00:25.440
of their attempted solutions.

01:00:25.440 --> 01:00:29.220
As already mentioned, the Classics pillar
includes Dragon Rush in the coveted bottom

01:00:29.220 --> 01:00:33.730
spot where you can actually interact without
needing to crank the camera or use the awkward

01:00:33.730 --> 01:00:35.460
skeuomorphic buttons.

01:00:35.460 --> 01:00:40.210
On the right hand side, though, is the Crowds
pillar, and for the last year one location

01:00:40.210 --> 01:00:42.460
has dominated that bottom slot.

01:00:42.460 --> 01:00:49.670
ICE Poker - The Stronghold, which routinely
boasts over 100 attendees, often pushing 200.

01:00:49.670 --> 01:00:54.961
Now, that number is middling in isolation,
but by the standards of Decentraland, where

01:00:54.961 --> 01:00:59.109
12 qualifies as a crowd, it’s pretty meaningful.

01:00:59.109 --> 01:01:03.030
Of course a ton of those attendees are idle
at the default spawn location, but let’s

01:01:03.030 --> 01:01:07.609
not ruin the fun.

01:01:07.609 --> 01:01:11.950
As for the legality of this, well, the opinion
of Decentra Games’ Singapore-based lawyers

01:01:11.950 --> 01:01:15.631
is that it’s not gambling under relevant
statutes, thus they don’t need a gaming

01:01:15.631 --> 01:01:17.079
license to operate.&nbsp;

01:01:17.079 --> 01:01:21.339
The important thing here is that the only
thing in Decentraland that seems to be actually

01:01:21.339 --> 01:01:25.869
successful is outright just a casino, and
regardless of the sentiment of Jacque Law

01:01:25.869 --> 01:01:30.760
LLC, the community seems to be taking it for
what it clearly is.

01:01:30.760 --> 01:01:36.250
“So as you can see I’ve earned $2.98 from
playing.

01:01:36.250 --> 01:01:41.099
And honestly I’ve only been gambli~oops
playing the casino for a couple of days.”

01:01:41.099 --> 01:01:45.020
The odd thing is that it’s seemingly only
the poker tables, at that.

01:01:45.020 --> 01:01:47.839
Like, really it’s just this one specific
casino.

01:01:47.839 --> 01:01:51.950
There’s a whole bunch of different casinos
around the gambling zone, basically all of

01:01:51.950 --> 01:01:57.369
them owned by DecentraGames under the ICE
brand, but The Stronghold is the only one

01:01:57.369 --> 01:01:59.710
anyone’s ever in.

01:01:59.710 --> 01:02:04.020
Turns out that’s because they already ran
into legal problems!

01:02:04.020 --> 01:02:08.650
You used to be able to gamble on the slots
at Tominoya with your cryptocurrency directly,

01:02:08.650 --> 01:02:12.940
but between glitchy behavior that would eat
transactions, high service fees, long wait

01:02:12.940 --> 01:02:17.730
times to resolve bets, and, most critically,
some pointed letters from various regulatory

01:02:17.730 --> 01:02:22.980
bodies, The Stronghold is the only location
that supports the current brown bag policy,

01:02:22.980 --> 01:02:27.109
and it only has poker, all the others have
been converted to free-play.

01:02:27.109 --> 01:02:31.549
You can still go over to The Aquarium and
play blackjack, but you will do so alone,

01:02:31.549 --> 01:02:35.589
these people are not here to play, they are
here to gamble.

01:02:35.589 --> 01:02:40.890
When discussing Decentraland, it’s hard
to overlook the subject of its abysmal user

01:02:40.890 --> 01:02:41.890
base.

01:02:41.890 --> 01:02:47.789
Indeed, the biggest story to come out of Decentraland
in 2022 was drama over DCL’s metrics.&nbsp;

01:02:47.789 --> 01:02:53.319
In October 2022, crypto outlet Coindesk reported
on DappRadar’s Data that claimed Decentraland

01:02:53.319 --> 01:02:56.750
had as few as 38 daily active users.

01:02:56.750 --> 01:03:01.160
Decentraland fired back in a blog post, scrutinizing
the data, calling it misinformation, and pushing

01:03:01.160 --> 01:03:03.319
their own value of 8,000.&nbsp;

01:03:03.319 --> 01:03:07.240
This back and forth continued, with Coindesk
releasing the ‘final word’ on the subject

01:03:07.240 --> 01:03:13.270
in January 2023 - with their research suggesting
an active daily user base of 810.

01:03:13.270 --> 01:03:20.109
Now, it’s tempting to fall into this whirlpool,
as apparently Coindesk did for 3 months, and

01:03:20.109 --> 01:03:25.299
litigate the definition of a “user,” but
let’s head this off at the pass.&nbsp;

01:03:25.299 --> 01:03:33.079
Whether Decentraland has daily activity of
40 users, 800 users or 8000 users is irrelevant,

01:03:33.079 --> 01:03:37.570
in their business these are all the same number.

01:03:37.570 --> 01:03:40.319
Emotionally it feels like a significant difference.

01:03:40.319 --> 01:03:42.480
But let’s give some context to these numbers.

01:03:42.480 --> 01:03:48.049
At 810 users, It's competing with Lord of
the Rings Online and it has a fraction of

01:03:48.049 --> 01:03:54.170
the users of Fishing Planet - which like,
it’s Fishing Planet.&nbsp;

01:03:54.170 --> 01:03:58.510
The most generous numbers for Decentraland
put it in competition with Star Wars: The

01:03:58.510 --> 01:04:00.230
Old Republic.

01:04:00.230 --> 01:04:04.140
All of these values are just Steam users,
it doesn’t include the users who access

01:04:04.140 --> 01:04:08.619
the games directly through their dedicated
launchers or on other platforms, so these

01:04:08.619 --> 01:04:12.020
numbers are inherently conservative.

01:04:12.020 --> 01:04:17.079
Despite having these varying player counts,
these games all have fundamentally the same

01:04:17.079 --> 01:04:18.309
business model.

01:04:18.309 --> 01:04:21.329
They are operating a micro-MMO.

01:04:21.329 --> 01:04:25.930
If you ever wonder how ‘dead MMOs’ survive,
it’s by running tight ships.

01:04:25.930 --> 01:04:31.240
These teams, often small, inherit a product
that was hugely expensive to produce five,

01:04:31.240 --> 01:04:35.680
ten, fifteen years ago, and they are tasked
with maintaining it in order to support a

01:04:35.680 --> 01:04:37.610
small but loyal user base.

01:04:37.610 --> 01:04:43.560
It is possible for Runes of Magic to be profitable
off the back of a single digit number of ‘whales’,

01:04:43.560 --> 01:04:47.050
people who just really like Runes of Magic.

01:04:47.050 --> 01:04:50.710
The same is true for Fiesta Online or LotRO.

01:04:50.710 --> 01:04:55.920
So it doesn’t matter what Decentraland’s
true daily user count looks like, because

01:04:55.920 --> 01:05:03.049
any number they’ve ever pushed falls into
the same band of ‘long, long dead MMO.’

01:05:03.049 --> 01:05:09.150
The fact that Decentraland has a similar user
base to DC Universe Online is the red flag

01:05:09.150 --> 01:05:11.500
that most people notice first.

01:05:11.500 --> 01:05:16.020
Daybreak Game Company is a game developer
that currently owns and maintains numerous

01:05:16.020 --> 01:05:17.549
old MMOs.

01:05:17.549 --> 01:05:21.619
Including Everquest, DC Universe Online, and
Lord of the Rings Online.

01:05:21.619 --> 01:05:27.549
This is a tough business, and in December
of 2020, Daybreak was acquired by Enad Global

01:05:27.549 --> 01:05:31.440
7, in an agreement valued at $300 million
dollars.

01:05:31.440 --> 01:05:37.770
So this company, that owns and maintains all
of these MMOs that are all individually comparable

01:05:37.770 --> 01:05:43.089
to Decentraland’s user base, is meant to
be worth a fraction of Decentraland?

01:05:43.089 --> 01:05:47.680
“And this is the story behind the company
worth nearly ten billion dollars at the market

01:05:47.680 --> 01:05:48.680
peak.”

01:05:48.680 --> 01:05:53.289
It’s been said numerous times before, but
it shouldn’t be left unsaid here.

01:05:53.289 --> 01:05:59.720
Decentraland’s purported value, based on
the market cap of its cryptocurrency, is completely

01:05:59.720 --> 01:06:01.770
disconnected from reality.

01:06:01.770 --> 01:06:04.359
Decentraland’s product is clearly trash.

01:06:04.359 --> 01:06:09.700
As a video game, it struggles to set itself
apart from Inferna, an MMO that advertises

01:06:09.700 --> 01:06:13.700
itself as an underdog off the back of its
“3 man dev team.”

01:06:13.700 --> 01:06:17.710
So Decentraland goes all-in on the speculation,
on narrative.

01:06:17.710 --> 01:06:21.609
Its value has nothing to do with what Decentraland
is today.

01:06:21.609 --> 01:06:31.589
But what it will be… and what it did do
when you weren’t watching.

01:06:31.589 --> 01:06:38.420
“Because the metaverse in its more mature
form will be a lot more advanced than this

01:06:38.420 --> 01:06:43.230
and a lot more sophisticated than this, but
this really is how it starts.

01:06:43.230 --> 01:06:46.360
This is the beginning of it and you know this
is going to be the shape of things for the

01:06:46.360 --> 01:06:49.559
next twenty to thirty years, I really believe
that.”

01:06:49.559 --> 01:06:55.540
The way that crypto users talk about Decentraland
is so dissociated from what you yourself witness,

01:06:55.540 --> 01:06:59.630
that it seems like they’re talking about
an entirely different thing.

01:06:59.630 --> 01:07:01.280
Like is this it?

01:07:01.280 --> 01:07:04.680
Is this the billion dollar virtual world?

01:07:04.680 --> 01:07:07.210
Surely I must be missing something.

01:07:07.210 --> 01:07:11.390
“I’m sorry you don’t like to make money
here in the network”

01:07:11.390 --> 01:07:15.670
Like so much of web3, Decentraland has weaponized
its speculative element.

01:07:15.670 --> 01:07:20.210
Since they are the future, the product as
it exists now borders on irrelevant.

01:07:20.210 --> 01:07:26.359
The proposed inevitability of the metaverse
is their greatest asset - because they don’t

01:07:26.359 --> 01:07:27.990
even need to try.

01:07:27.990 --> 01:07:33.730
This is the everything-proof shield that Decentraland
users have created to guard from criticism.

01:07:33.730 --> 01:07:39.109
Decentraland and all its contents is forever
a prototype - always just an indication of

01:07:39.109 --> 01:07:40.930
what the future will bring.

01:07:40.930 --> 01:07:43.730
Being bad is not a fatal flaw in the crypto
space.

01:07:43.730 --> 01:07:47.350
Just look at the Red Ape Family, they’re
visibly incompetent and they still made it

01:07:47.350 --> 01:07:52.250
to three episodes, even if the creator is
too much of a coward to post the third.

01:07:52.250 --> 01:07:57.349
Every successful crypto project is bad, but
with enough legitimacy you can mask that fact.

01:07:57.349 --> 01:08:01.760
This generates extremely funny scenarios,
like crypto shill Robin from The Defiant stumbling

01:08:01.760 --> 01:08:05.950
through Decentraland’s barren wasteland
while trying to invent reasons why what he’s

01:08:05.950 --> 01:08:08.359
looking at isn’t plainly obviously ass.

01:08:08.359 --> 01:08:15.680
“And I often say that the Metaverse is…
it’s a world that rotates around you.

01:08:15.680 --> 01:08:20.880
And this is what I mean, the world literally
does rotate around you, this is… it’s

01:08:20.880 --> 01:08:23.170
a universe where you’re always at the center.&nbsp;

01:08:23.170 --> 01:08:30.960
That is the whole kind of mindset change that
you have to get when you talk about the metaverse.”

01:08:30.960 --> 01:08:36.600
Decentraland users understand this need for
legitimacy intuitively, and so it becomes

01:08:36.600 --> 01:08:38.440
what they crave above all else.

01:08:38.440 --> 01:08:43.190
That is why they value brand activations so
highly, no matter the circumstances.

01:08:43.190 --> 01:08:47.719
Being able to put “Vodafone” on their
world map, or “Pedigree” in their events

01:08:47.719 --> 01:08:52.320
tab, spins the narrative that real companies
are here in Decentraland.

01:08:52.320 --> 01:08:56.960
Even embarrassing brand activations are defended,
because “they’re just dipping their toes

01:08:56.960 --> 01:08:59.839
in, this is just the beginning.”

01:08:59.839 --> 01:09:03.920
Decentraland users seek to encourage brand
activations and events through pathetic pandering.

01:09:03.920 --> 01:09:08.339
They’ll smile through gritted teeth as they
insist that the Hershey’s Experience really

01:09:08.339 --> 01:09:10.679
is interesting and successful.

01:09:10.679 --> 01:09:15.440
By performatively engaging with events and
brand activations, users seek to create the

01:09:15.440 --> 01:09:20.589
illusion that these events are successful
in the hopes of drawing in the next sucker.

01:09:20.589 --> 01:09:21.600
“What’s cooking good looking people.

01:09:21.600 --> 01:09:23.680
We’re in Decentraland and we’re in Hersheyverse.

01:09:23.680 --> 01:09:25.560
Can you believe that?

01:09:25.560 --> 01:09:26.970
Hershey’s bro, Hershey.

01:09:26.970 --> 01:09:29.310
However you say that, Hershey.

01:09:29.310 --> 01:09:32.590
Pretty sure it’s Hershey, the fuck bro Mandela?

01:09:32.590 --> 01:09:33.590
Bro.

01:09:33.590 --> 01:09:37.630
Who woulda thought that Hershey was gonna
make a little plot on Decentraland.

01:09:37.630 --> 01:09:40.359
You know before you know it you’re gonna
see other candy companies.

01:09:40.359 --> 01:09:43.880
You know there’s already beverage companies,
there’s already alcohol companies, there’s

01:09:43.880 --> 01:09:49.560
fucking Yahoo, Amazon is already in here,
and Hershey is the next big one to get into

01:09:49.560 --> 01:09:50.560
this shit.”

01:09:50.560 --> 01:09:53.429
This principle scales up from the Pedigree
Fosterverse all the way up to the marquee

01:09:53.429 --> 01:09:54.920
events of the platform.

01:09:54.920 --> 01:09:58.790
The Metaverse Music Festival, which in its
most recent outing was tossed around as having

01:09:58.790 --> 01:10:04.280
featured performances from Bjork, Ozzy Ozzbourne,
and Mötorhead, consisted almost entirely

01:10:04.280 --> 01:10:10.060
of pre-recorded messages from the musicians
followed by just playing a couple music videos.

01:10:10.060 --> 01:10:13.080
“It’s awesome to be in the Metaverse with
you and it feels great to be here in Decentraland.

01:10:13.080 --> 01:10:14.080
I hope you’re all having an amazing time
today.

01:10:14.080 --> 01:10:15.199
My name is Dave Mustaine from Megadeath and
today I’ll be playing you a few of my favorite

01:10:15.199 --> 01:10:16.199
tracks.

01:10:16.199 --> 01:10:17.199
Let me know what you think about this one.”

01:10:17.199 --> 01:10:31.200
[Opening chords of The Sick, The Dying, and
The Dead]

01:10:31.200 --> 01:10:35.830
I criticized Fortnite for being overly poetic
with its definition of a “live” Marshmallow

01:10:35.830 --> 01:10:40.020
concert, and this is just, this is just so
much worse.

01:10:40.020 --> 01:10:44.449
The Metaverse Fashion Week, as the name implies,
was a week-long fashion event held in Decentraland

01:10:44.449 --> 01:10:49.020
in March of 2022, scheduled to run just after
the Milan Fashion Week.

01:10:49.020 --> 01:10:53.490
The event was organized by the Decentraland
Foundation, the developers of Decentraland.

01:10:53.490 --> 01:10:57.460
It was the involvement of the Foundation that
saw the Metaverse Fashion Week get some real

01:10:57.460 --> 01:10:59.280
attention and media coverage.

01:10:59.280 --> 01:11:03.560
A number of lifestyle, fashion, and tech journalists
were sent in to cover the event, for whom

01:11:03.560 --> 01:11:07.110
many were experiencing the metaverse for the
first time.&nbsp;

01:11:07.110 --> 01:11:11.550
The event was billed as an opportunity to
see the next major innovation in the fashion

01:11:11.550 --> 01:11:12.550
industry.

01:11:12.550 --> 01:11:15.880
A new way for fashion to be designed, bought
and sold.

01:11:15.880 --> 01:11:19.000
It would be the melding of fashion and art.

01:11:19.000 --> 01:11:22.710
Finally, art and fashion - together at last.

01:11:22.710 --> 01:11:27.580
The word ‘phygital’ got thrown around
a lot, to refer to the simultaneous purchasing

01:11:27.580 --> 01:11:29.489
of a digital and physical good.

01:11:29.489 --> 01:11:33.500
Like you buy a digital hat in Decentraland,
and a real hat turns up in the mail.

01:11:33.500 --> 01:11:37.890
Not to get distracted, but World of Warcraft
did this exact promotion in 2010.

01:11:37.890 --> 01:11:42.290
This plush Wind Rider Cub came with an in-game
pet, it’s not a new thing.

01:11:42.290 --> 01:11:45.600
But of course, they don’t frame it that
way; instead you’re buying a single hat

01:11:45.600 --> 01:11:48.090
with two simultaneous planes of existence.

01:11:48.090 --> 01:11:53.810
It’s not a bundle of two products, it’s
the same hat in two different forms.

01:11:53.810 --> 01:11:58.199
There are dozens of tech startups eager to
composite clothing over photo or video and

01:11:58.199 --> 01:12:01.130
label it as the future of self-expression.

01:12:01.130 --> 01:12:06.280
This so-called ‘screen wear’ is at its
best, an artful photoshop job, and at worst,

01:12:06.280 --> 01:12:08.730
essentially just a Tiktok or Zoom filter.&nbsp;

01:12:08.730 --> 01:12:10.290
“There are different avenues.

01:12:10.290 --> 01:12:15.320
So the first thing is you could wear it on
your real self through an AR filter.

01:12:15.320 --> 01:12:20.389
So that’s number one, and that’s the part
where technology is still, you know, a work

01:12:20.389 --> 01:12:21.389
in progress.

01:12:21.389 --> 01:12:22.690
It's going to get better and better, we know
it.

01:12:22.690 --> 01:12:28.680
&nbsp;So I think as we, regular people, start
transitioning more and more into the virtual

01:12:28.680 --> 01:12:34.480
world, spending more time in virtual environments
and that barrier between physical and digital

01:12:34.480 --> 01:12:40.639
starts blurring more and more, and we will
start consuming digital fashion, digital sneakers,

01:12:40.639 --> 01:12:44.860
digital makeup, digital jewelry” 
This was the rhetoric that defined the Metaverse

01:12:44.860 --> 01:12:45.940
Fashion Week.&nbsp;

01:12:45.940 --> 01:12:50.110
So, our ragtag ensemble of journalists and
creators were sent in to report on the future

01:12:50.110 --> 01:12:51.730
of reality and fashion.

01:12:51.730 --> 01:12:53.090
How did it go?

01:12:53.090 --> 01:12:56.350
Do you really need to be told at this point?

01:12:56.350 --> 01:13:03.030
[annoying sounds from DCL]&nbsp;

01:13:03.030 --> 01:13:10.890
“What the fuck did I just watch?”

01:13:10.890 --> 01:13:16.030
“After a pre-show trial session spent exploring
Decentraland’s ‘Luxury Fashion District’—maybe

01:13:16.030 --> 01:13:20.360
it was too early, but the only fashion space
I teleported to was an empty DKNY store.

01:13:20.360 --> 01:13:24.850
“While there were plenty of fashion shows,
very few were successful because the overall

01:13:24.850 --> 01:13:26.610
experience fell flat.

01:13:26.610 --> 01:13:31.389
Perhaps expectedly, many weren’t able to
capture the magic of a physical show, where

01:13:31.389 --> 01:13:35.639
attendees are fully immersed and emotionally
invested in the brand’s collection.

01:13:35.639 --> 01:13:39.850
The game-ified aesthetic in Decentraland took
away from the fantasy and craftsmanship of

01:13:39.850 --> 01:13:45.820
luxury fashion and, simply put, the avatar
models weren’t as inspiring.

01:13:45.820 --> 01:13:54.850
“I don’t know how I feel about it.

01:13:54.850 --> 01:14:00.100
I literally canceled everything including
my workout to watch this show.”

01:14:00.100 --> 01:14:04.060
And there was just very little documenting
of the event in general.

01:14:04.060 --> 01:14:08.870
This is due to a combination of disinterest,
but also a deliberate effort not to showcase

01:14:08.870 --> 01:14:10.670
the embarrassing product.

01:14:10.670 --> 01:14:15.260
Even the brands involved in the show downplayed
the Decentraland part of their Decentraland

01:14:15.260 --> 01:14:16.260
show.

01:14:16.260 --> 01:14:19.820
“The shows didn’t look the way the brands
expected they would, as publications on their

01:14:19.820 --> 01:14:21.239
social networks made clear.

01:14:21.239 --> 01:14:25.760
This is because brands posted the best photos
from the show and not the reality of pixelated

01:14:25.760 --> 01:14:27.380
avatars and simple looks.”

01:14:27.380 --> 01:14:32.810
Likewise, musicians who “performed” at
the music festival didn’t exactly rush to

01:14:32.810 --> 01:14:39.040
show off the event, either, so footage of
any of these theoretically big events is shockingly

01:14:39.040 --> 01:14:40.040
sparse.

01:14:40.040 --> 01:14:45.000
This isn’t some grand conspiracy, it’s
just dozens of individuals independently reaching

01:14:45.000 --> 01:14:49.790
the same conclusion: the product sucks and
it’d be a bad idea to show it.

01:14:49.790 --> 01:14:54.060
This is true for both DCL users, but also
the journalists covering it.

01:14:54.060 --> 01:14:59.910
Decentraland looks so unprofessional that
coverage of DCL looks unprofessional by extension.

01:14:59.910 --> 01:15:03.540
“And this actually looks, it looks sorta
like me.

01:15:03.540 --> 01:15:06.440
I like it so much I’m going to dance for
you.”

01:15:06.440 --> 01:15:09.420
“We do have a whisky distillery”

01:15:09.420 --> 01:15:11.400
“You’ve been holding out.

01:15:11.400 --> 01:15:12.659
Oh, this is cool.”

01:15:12.659 --> 01:15:17.090
“Here we can walk around and actually see
the distillery from the inside.”

01:15:17.090 --> 01:15:21.680
“I enjoy your virtual distillery, not so
much your virtual bourbon, so cheers Gigi.”

01:15:21.680 --> 01:15:23.750
“Cheers to that!”

01:15:23.750 --> 01:15:33.190
“I mean, so here’s the thing, uh, is it
for us, I don’t know, but there are hundreds

01:15:33.190 --> 01:15:38.060
of thousands of people who already live in
the metaverse, who exist in the metaverse,

01:15:38.060 --> 01:15:40.600
who are buying things or hanging out.”

01:15:40.600 --> 01:15:44.890
This creates an environment where the Metaverse
Fashion Week was a thing talked about in the

01:15:44.890 --> 01:15:45.890
abstract.

01:15:45.890 --> 01:15:51.150
It was a thing that did happen, but the reality
is slowly eroded away.

01:15:51.150 --> 01:15:56.480
Most people who learned about the event didn’t
see the product, they only heard the narrative.

01:15:56.480 --> 01:15:59.239
And that narrative went a little something
like this:

01:15:59.239 --> 01:16:03.489
“The wearables were supposed to be available
immediately after the runway event at a nearby

01:16:03.489 --> 01:16:09.330
pop-up shop, and for free — a rarity considering
fashion’s rush to capitalize on crypto clothing.

01:16:09.330 --> 01:16:13.750
For some reason, a couple of hours after it
ended, they weren’t displayed yet inside

01:16:13.750 --> 01:16:15.370
the paisley-patterned store.

01:16:15.370 --> 01:16:17.630
A good reason to come back.”

01:16:17.630 --> 01:16:21.920
This journalist witnessed a broken feature,
they experienced a bug.

01:16:21.920 --> 01:16:25.980
Rather than reporting critically on the failure
of a key feature of the event, the article

01:16:25.980 --> 01:16:29.440
pulls its punch in the last line through the
language of puff journalism.

01:16:29.440 --> 01:16:32.699
Out of context, it reads almost like satire.

01:16:32.699 --> 01:16:37.050
But if you read enough coverage of Decentraland,
this becomes a very real trend.

01:16:37.050 --> 01:16:40.930
“Last of all, I can definitely see this
event evolving into something much more engaging.

01:16:40.930 --> 01:16:45.200
there are communities of people who are replicating
in various metaverse outposts behaviors that

01:16:45.200 --> 01:16:49.080
people (or people dressed as elves) have long
cultivated in reality.

01:16:49.080 --> 01:16:51.180
And fashion is one of those behaviors.

01:16:51.180 --> 01:16:54.430
So we’re all just going to need a better
graphics card.

01:16:54.430 --> 01:16:59.540
“However, MVFW did provide a glimpse into
possibilities for how brands can build their

01:16:59.540 --> 01:17:04.949
narratives in the metaverse—offering immersive
experiences and innovative ways to expand

01:17:04.949 --> 01:17:06.490
offerings beyond the physical.&nbsp;

01:17:06.490 --> 01:17:12.190
“Once VR glasses are integrated the experience
is going to be on a whole other level.”

01:17:12.190 --> 01:17:16.331
And in the absence of any voices to the contrary,
that becomes the narrative of the Metaverse

01:17:16.331 --> 01:17:17.350
Fashion Week.

01:17:17.350 --> 01:17:21.650
It had ‘some technical issues’, ‘reviews
were mixed’, but it was a proof of concept

01:17:21.650 --> 01:17:25.340
that left journalists feeling certain that
the concept has a future.

01:17:25.340 --> 01:17:29.280
And through that lens, that allows those involved
to say with a straight face that the event

01:17:29.280 --> 01:17:30.540
was a success.

01:17:30.540 --> 01:17:33.290
“It was a fantastic four days.

01:17:33.290 --> 01:17:39.540
As a proof-point of what is to come; this
is the early evolution of decentralized commerce”

01:17:39.540 --> 01:17:44.239
On the whole, journalists are seemingly overwhelmed
by the tempest of claims and ideas surrounding

01:17:44.239 --> 01:17:45.239
the metaverse.

01:17:45.239 --> 01:17:50.280
We can appreciate why this is the case; a
lot of this is vague either on purpose or

01:17:50.280 --> 01:17:51.280
on accident.

01:17:51.280 --> 01:17:56.180
It is not the job of the writers at Women’s
Wear Daily to crack crypto wide open.&nbsp;

01:17:56.180 --> 01:18:01.429
It’s all too big, too out of scope, they’re
convinced that they “just don’t get it.”

01:18:01.429 --> 01:18:06.750
So they defer to the explanations and narratives
given to them by people trying to sell something.

01:18:06.750 --> 01:18:09.969
“I mean, so here’s the thing, I…”

01:18:09.969 --> 01:18:14.139
Eric Ravenscraft has discussed this phenomenon
with a specific eye towards journalists in

01:18:14.139 --> 01:18:19.239
outlets like the New York Times uncritically
parroting the vocabulary of real estate for

01:18:19.239 --> 01:18:26.500
the digital groundbreaking of metaverse headquarters
that in many cases don’t even actually exist.

01:18:26.500 --> 01:18:28.370
&nbsp;
This goes all the way back to the first major

01:18:28.370 --> 01:18:34.460
coverage given to Decentraland - A BBC story
in 2018 in response to the initial land auction.

01:18:34.460 --> 01:18:40.050
“In Decentraland users can build whatever
they can imagine on plots of virtual land.

01:18:40.050 --> 01:18:46.140
But the creators are giving BBC Tending a
sneak peek.”

01:18:46.140 --> 01:18:50.850
What’s important to remember here is that
the Decentraland we’re seeing wouldn’t

01:18:50.850 --> 01:18:56.970
exist for another 2 years, the actual product
in 2018 looked like this.

01:18:56.970 --> 01:19:02.020
So by its very nature there is nothing really
to show.

01:19:02.020 --> 01:19:05.830
And it means we get these lovely little artifacts
from the past.

01:19:05.830 --> 01:19:14.080
“Right, whoah, I seem to be on some sort
of dance floor, I can hear music, it’s very

01:19:14.080 --> 01:19:15.080
colourful.

01:19:15.080 --> 01:19:19.110
So there seems to be a zombie out in the distance.

01:19:19.110 --> 01:19:20.870
Oh gosh, he’s coming right at me!”

01:19:20.870 --> 01:19:24.909
“It’ll be pretty much exactly like Facebook
except you’ll be able to hear and see each

01:19:24.909 --> 01:19:25.909
other in VR.

01:19:25.909 --> 01:19:30.320
So a bit more of a real experience.

01:19:30.320 --> 01:19:33.830
As long as we’ve got the internet, stick
our headsets on, and I’ll be able to have

01:19:33.830 --> 01:19:36.330
friends come round my house in Decentraland,
or I can go to their house for a virtual cup

01:19:36.330 --> 01:19:37.330
of tea.”

01:19:37.330 --> 01:19:40.159
“How high would the price have to get for
you to sell that land?”

01:19:40.159 --> 01:19:44.300
“I’m not selling it until it’s worth
at least $10 million.”

01:19:44.300 --> 01:19:45.300
“Ten million?”

01:19:45.300 --> 01:19:46.300
“Yeah.”

01:19:46.300 --> 01:19:48.100
He sold for $980 a year later.

01:19:48.100 --> 01:19:49.570
“A million dollars!

01:19:49.570 --> 01:19:50.570
But it doesn’t exist?”

01:19:50.570 --> 01:19:55.410
“It does here, and it will do, in about
a year I believe.”

01:19:55.410 --> 01:20:00.270
The spine of this story is built not on what
Decentraland did, or was doing, but what it

01:20:00.270 --> 01:20:01.370
will do.

01:20:01.370 --> 01:20:06.360
All of the features that will be implemented
and benchmarks it will hit.

01:20:06.360 --> 01:20:10.989
Journalists and vloggers who very understandably
don’t know the deep lore of Decentraland’s

01:20:10.989 --> 01:20:15.891
development still mention VR as a thing that
just hasn’t been implemented yet, despite

01:20:15.891 --> 01:20:17.360
the feature being scrapped.

01:20:17.360 --> 01:20:23.580
“Once VR glasses are integrated the experience
is going to be on a whole other level.”

01:20:23.580 --> 01:20:26.790
Decentraland craves legitimacy, but it can
subsist on crumbs.

01:20:26.790 --> 01:20:31.460
It is a speculative real estate scheme which
is rapidly approaching the Nigerian Prince

01:20:31.460 --> 01:20:33.250
level of self-selection.

01:20:33.250 --> 01:20:38.679
That means that for its purpose, all it needs
is for people to discuss it, and for the conversation

01:20:38.679 --> 01:20:41.200
to feature the defenses we’ve been discussing.

01:20:41.200 --> 01:20:47.290
It’s just a prototype, it will improve,
the metaverse is inevitable.

01:20:47.290 --> 01:20:52.460
Because this reporting loads the bases for
investment advice like this to sound believable.

01:20:52.460 --> 01:20:57.940
“Land is hitting all time highs for value
and people are asking ‘can people really

01:20:57.940 --> 01:21:00.890
afford to buy these plots for a lot?

01:21:00.890 --> 01:21:04.731
Yes because there’s going to be large commercial
and business entities that are going to be

01:21:04.731 --> 01:21:07.821
able to make a lot more than what they’re
going to pay for this land - they know it.

01:21:07.821 --> 01:21:12.540
It has long term ramifications in the industry,
so it’s a great investment.”

01:21:12.540 --> 01:21:16.659
If you took Kevin’s advice at the time,
you’d be in the red less than two weeks

01:21:16.659 --> 01:21:23.150
later, have lost half your investment 2 months
later and be down 80 to 90% today.

01:21:23.150 --> 01:21:28.100
That is the danger posed by nonchalant coverage
of Decentraland, boosting the narrative that

01:21:28.100 --> 01:21:31.780
major brands and mass adoption are right around
the corner.&nbsp;

01:21:31.780 --> 01:21:36.370
There’s no such thing as bad publicity for
Decentraland.

01:21:36.370 --> 01:21:41.440
It continues to subsist through performative
hype and deception.&nbsp;

01:21:41.440 --> 01:21:47.030
Decentraland needs to be recognized for what
it is, and can’t be given the easy way out.

01:21:47.030 --> 01:21:51.560
Decentraland is not, and has never been, a
VR experience.

01:21:51.560 --> 01:21:55.480
You cannot order a pizza in Decentraland.

01:21:55.480 --> 01:21:57.440
Decentraland is not the future.

01:21:57.440 --> 01:22:12.350
It has never, and will never be, worth a billion
dollars.

01:22:12.350 --> 01:22:17.280
So at this point I think it’s plain to see
that the product of Decentraland is, at best,

01:22:17.280 --> 01:22:18.280
bad.

01:22:18.280 --> 01:22:22.520
But Decentraland users are battle-hardened
on this point - they’ve had years of defending

01:22:22.520 --> 01:22:24.139
against this claim.

01:22:24.139 --> 01:22:29.219
Many DCL users will concede that sure, the
product itself is janky, but obviously its

01:22:29.219 --> 01:22:33.520
just a prototype - and the real appeal of
Decentraland, what sets it apart from its

01:22:33.520 --> 01:22:37.200
competition, is its system of governance.

01:22:37.200 --> 01:22:41.139
Decentraland was built and is maintained by
‘the Foundation’, the actual developers

01:22:41.139 --> 01:22:42.139
behind the product.

01:22:42.139 --> 01:22:46.650
Meilich and Ordano always intended for the
Foundation to go hands-off, simply becoming

01:22:46.650 --> 01:22:51.510
the underlying machinery of the world akin
to the invisibility of a web host.

01:22:51.510 --> 01:22:55.630
As part of this invisibility, they wanted
users to handle as many elements of development

01:22:55.630 --> 01:22:57.480
and maintenance as possible.

01:22:57.480 --> 01:23:02.030
This is characterized through the language
of ownership - Decentraland is “owned by

01:23:02.030 --> 01:23:07.480
its users”, so naturally the users have
direct control over the product.

01:23:07.480 --> 01:23:12.020
This is further obfuscated through the language
of Decentraland as a reified space - which

01:23:12.020 --> 01:23:17.800
is to say, Decentraland users present themselves
as akin to the government of a virtual world

01:23:17.800 --> 01:23:20.590
- enacting policies in a democratic fashion.

01:23:20.590 --> 01:23:23.969
They do this through the Decentraland DAO.

01:23:23.969 --> 01:23:28.820
Because the Decentraland DAO dresses itself
in the language of governance and law, it’s

01:23:28.820 --> 01:23:32.560
tempting to engage with them on that level
and take the DAO seriously.

01:23:32.560 --> 01:23:36.830
We tried to give them a fair go, but we found
ourselves repeating the mistakes of the journalists

01:23:36.830 --> 01:23:38.360
we just criticized.

01:23:38.360 --> 01:23:42.640
To contrast them against real governments
implies that the comparison is warranted,

01:23:42.640 --> 01:23:46.930
and humors the notion that these are adults
attempting to revolutionize the concept of

01:23:46.930 --> 01:23:47.930
governance.

01:23:47.930 --> 01:23:52.120
So let’s adjust expectations and lead with
the soul of the DAO.

01:23:52.120 --> 01:23:56.120
Upon revisiting the DAO’s governance site
for this project, the first thing that caught

01:23:56.120 --> 01:23:57.600
our eye was this.&nbsp;

01:23:57.600 --> 01:24:01.449
A proposal to fire a Committee member for
abuse of power and bias.

01:24:01.449 --> 01:24:05.880
And let me tell you, the contents of this
would be right at home on Something Awful

01:24:05.880 --> 01:24:07.050
circa 2008.

01:24:07.050 --> 01:24:12.760
A guy got banned on Discord and got so upset
that he wrote a 1000-word essay on a sock

01:24:12.760 --> 01:24:17.210
puppet about why the moderator should be fired
for abusing their authority.

01:24:17.210 --> 01:24:21.510
Because when asked, the moderator kinda said
he was a pluralist and “pluralists hate

01:24:21.510 --> 01:24:22.659
the individual.”

01:24:22.659 --> 01:24:24.850
And on and on it goes.

01:24:24.850 --> 01:24:30.190
The proposal was rejected, but it was rejected
as an invalid question because the Team Manager

01:24:30.190 --> 01:24:35.200
of the DAO Facilitation Squad does not count
as a member of the DAO Committee, and thus

01:24:35.200 --> 01:24:40.290
a complaint would need to be made to the Grant
Support Squad who would then need to recommend

01:24:40.290 --> 01:24:45.860
a proposal to revoke the grant funding the
DAO Facilitation Squad, thus firing Matimio

01:24:45.860 --> 01:24:47.340
indirectly.

01:24:47.340 --> 01:24:51.090
Then there’s Kevin’s failed grant proposal
- where multiple people rejected the proposal

01:24:51.090 --> 01:24:54.940
at least in part because Kevin blocked them
on Twitter.

01:24:54.940 --> 01:24:58.530
When you block someone on Twitter, this is
where they end up.

01:24:58.530 --> 01:25:04.530
This is a group of people that sees the Sugar
Club, the empty bar next to Hershey’s, requesting

01:25:04.530 --> 01:25:11.909
$60,000 US dollars to fund its “operating
costs” - and they’re like yeah that seems

01:25:11.909 --> 01:25:12.909
reasonable.

01:25:12.909 --> 01:25:16.820
When they hold elections, the candidates are
asked questions like: “What are each candidate's

01:25:16.820 --> 01:25:21.739
stance on bringing physical world ideologies
into our decentralized world?

01:25:21.739 --> 01:25:25.809
You know, government structures - democracy,
communism, whatever.”&nbsp;

01:25:25.809 --> 01:25:30.659
In that example, the candidates universally
accepted the idea that DCL was apolitical

01:25:30.659 --> 01:25:35.170
- and they would support politics being patched
into Decentraland, but only if it achieved

01:25:35.170 --> 01:25:37.449
majority support in an official vote.

01:25:37.449 --> 01:25:43.469
“This is like a grand experiment, however
there’s a lot at stake so I think any ideology

01:25:43.469 --> 01:25:51.139
that we’re considering bringing in it would
be best handled on a side-project.”

01:25:51.139 --> 01:25:55.420
My dudes, the ideology is coming from inside
the house.

01:25:55.420 --> 01:25:59.639
Their philosophy of governance minimizes individual
authority and decision-making.

01:25:59.639 --> 01:26:03.790
But they pass phaux-legislation that gives
committee members absolute discretion with

01:26:03.790 --> 01:26:05.620
no appeals process.

01:26:05.620 --> 01:26:10.200
The Decentraland DAO is no less of a failure
than the Hershey’s Experience or DecentRally

01:26:10.200 --> 01:26:16.090
- we’re just changing gears from bad video
games to bureaucratic incompetence.

01:26:16.090 --> 01:26:22.280
You can’t really understand the DAO until
you wrap your head around just how inequitable

01:26:22.280 --> 01:26:24.700
the voting situation is.

01:26:24.700 --> 01:26:27.720
DAOs often makes use of weighted voting schemes.

01:26:27.720 --> 01:26:32.340
The main purpose of this is to ‘acknowledge
a user’s stake in the project’ - the logic

01:26:32.340 --> 01:26:36.970
being that those with the most invested have
the most at stake in the DAO.

01:26:36.970 --> 01:26:41.820
Though the details can vary between DAO’s,
Decentraland’s scheme is refreshingly straight

01:26:41.820 --> 01:26:42.820
forward.

01:26:42.820 --> 01:26:47.010
Votes are weighted based on the sum of Decentraland
tokens in the attached wallet.

01:26:47.010 --> 01:26:49.679
1 MANA is worth 1 point of Vote Power.

01:26:49.679 --> 01:26:55.460
It’s just wealth, the more money you have,
the more your vote counts - one to one.

01:26:55.460 --> 01:26:59.830
Naturally, this leads to those with wealth
shutting down any proposal that bothers them.

01:26:59.830 --> 01:27:01.739
For instance - Let’s look at this proposal.

01:27:01.739 --> 01:27:05.470
“Should we address the Voting Power Distribution
in the DAO?”

01:27:05.470 --> 01:27:09.869
In the proposal, a DAO Committee member pointed
out the obvious issues of the system.

01:27:09.869 --> 01:27:11.720
And the results turned out like this.

01:27:11.720 --> 01:27:15.080
163 votes for, 45 against.

01:27:15.080 --> 01:27:20.340
That is 78% support in terms of ‘real’
votes, though we’ll get to that, but once

01:27:20.340 --> 01:27:26.230
Voting Power was accounted for, the margin
inverted, to 73% against.

01:27:26.230 --> 01:27:34.310
And then even within that, of the 5.18 million
VP against the proposal, 4 million came from

01:27:34.310 --> 01:27:35.770
a single wallet.

01:27:35.770 --> 01:27:44.139
4 wallets make up almost all of the votes
against the proposal - 99.7% of the VP in

01:27:44.139 --> 01:27:45.990
the 73% majority.

01:27:45.990 --> 01:27:50.060
The next step would naturally be to play with
these figures versus the real votes, you know

01:27:50.060 --> 01:27:55.730
‘2% of voters accounted for 73% of the VP’
- but the issue there is that even though

01:27:55.730 --> 01:28:03.050
this weighted system allegedly exists to combat
bots, users still use bots for the optics.

01:28:03.050 --> 01:28:09.970
When you actually look at the votes, 128 of
the 209 votes were from accounts with 0 VP.

01:28:09.970 --> 01:28:13.540
These were accounts with literally no influence
on the vote.

01:28:13.540 --> 01:28:16.940
80% of those dummy votes were for Yes.

01:28:16.940 --> 01:28:23.590
19 of these accounts only had a single VP,
and then a bunch had 2 VP - both equally capable

01:28:23.590 --> 01:28:25.440
of being socket puppets.

01:28:25.440 --> 01:28:31.949
But whether Guest#60fd is a real user or not
is irrelevant - because one wallet lapped

01:28:31.949 --> 01:28:33.220
the competition.

01:28:33.220 --> 01:28:44.440
One wallet told the DAO go to [static] itself,
just really [static], and the DAO politely

01:28:44.440 --> 01:28:45.699
complied.

01:28:45.699 --> 01:28:50.340
These whales are often burner accounts who
are not available for negotiation,&nbsp; their

01:28:50.340 --> 01:28:53.440
decrees are silent, yet drown out all other
voices.

01:28:53.440 --> 01:28:59.699
But there is still an urge to pretend like
negotiation is a possibility.

01:28:59.699 --> 01:29:01.440
“But we still did get it denied.

01:29:01.440 --> 01:29:07.310
Although we met the threshold of approval
in the final hours we did get 3 votes which

01:29:07.310 --> 01:29:13.489
came in to deny and sort of made us step back
and say we need to re-evaluate how we’re

01:29:13.489 --> 01:29:18.840
bringing this forward because ultimately even
reaching the VP threshold of approval by the

01:29:18.840 --> 01:29:24.090
community, it seems like the whales still
felt like something else needed to be addressed

01:29:24.090 --> 01:29:27.489
outside of the other feedback that we had.”

01:29:27.489 --> 01:29:32.409
Kevin is clearly upset by this, and despite
all his talk about “addressing the concerns

01:29:32.409 --> 01:29:35.619
of the whales”, he understands that he’s
talking crap.

01:29:35.619 --> 01:29:40.889
He wants the DAO to give him money, the anonymous
whales in question do not want him to receive

01:29:40.889 --> 01:29:41.889
money.

01:29:41.889 --> 01:29:46.170
There is no negotiating or compromising, just
pharaohs you can pray to.

01:29:46.170 --> 01:29:49.600
So that leads us naturally onto the subject
of the DAO’s management.

01:29:49.600 --> 01:29:53.530
The DAO has been speed-running bureaucratic
paralysis.

01:29:53.530 --> 01:29:58.640
In 2020 they attempted to automate governance
by running an on-chain system, but it didn’t

01:29:58.640 --> 01:30:03.770
work, no one wanted to pay transaction costs
to vote on every name ban and point of interest,

01:30:03.770 --> 01:30:07.460
so they moved the system back off-chain six
months later.

01:30:07.460 --> 01:30:12.400
Problem is that without automation you need
humans to push the buttons.

01:30:12.400 --> 01:30:15.940
Decentraland is quite proud of the DAO’s
grant scheme - but it’s been a massive headache

01:30:15.940 --> 01:30:17.420
for them recently.

01:30:17.420 --> 01:30:21.920
For the first year they gave away 3.5 million
dollars in grants to develop projects that

01:30:21.920 --> 01:30:27.350
would aid adoption, but the grant application
had no standard process, and grants had no

01:30:27.350 --> 01:30:33.020
oversight and no accountability - because
no one existed with the authority to do it.

01:30:33.020 --> 01:30:38.140
The initial proposal alluded to the idea of
revoking grants - but included no framework

01:30:38.140 --> 01:30:39.420
to implement it.

01:30:39.420 --> 01:30:43.400
So the DAO Committee put forward a proposal
to set up a Squad whose job would be to help

01:30:43.400 --> 01:30:47.860
grantees deliver their product - and in the
extreme case where a project had collapsed

01:30:47.860 --> 01:30:52.929
or was too far off course, the Squad could
recommend that the Committee revoke the grant.

01:30:52.929 --> 01:30:57.350
But the Committee is administrative, they
can’t revoke the grant, and the Squad are

01:30:57.350 --> 01:31:02.360
essentially independent contractors, they
can’t revoke the grant either, so we need

01:31:02.360 --> 01:31:07.350
a new committee with express permission to
revoke grants.

01:31:07.350 --> 01:31:09.650
Enter the Grant Revocation Committee.

01:31:09.650 --> 01:31:14.340
We could go crazy with dissecting this, but
the short version is that these committees

01:31:14.340 --> 01:31:17.060
are meant to have minimal discretion.

01:31:17.060 --> 01:31:22.000
The authors of the policy seem to think discretion
only exists if you acknowledge it.

01:31:22.000 --> 01:31:27.969
So rather than limiting the scope of consideration
like any other discretionary position in government,

01:31:27.969 --> 01:31:33.240
they limit the committee to only considering
cases that are actively handed to them, but

01:31:33.240 --> 01:31:39.560
put no restraints on the committee members’
actual decision making effectively giving

01:31:39.560 --> 01:31:45.000
them absolute power to revoke any grant for
any reason.&nbsp;

01:31:45.000 --> 01:31:49.270
And as the cherry on top, there’s no appeals
process.

01:31:49.270 --> 01:31:53.790
Because to appeal the verdict would require
a different group with the authority to overturn

01:31:53.790 --> 01:31:56.340
the decision of the Revocation Committee.&nbsp;

01:31:56.340 --> 01:32:03.960
The committee intended to have minimal discretion
is completely unrestrained with zero accountability.

01:32:03.960 --> 01:32:11.850
Oh and these guys are paid commission for
every verdict - so the DAO is about to learn

01:32:11.850 --> 01:32:14.880
real fast why we don’t do that in the real
world.

01:32:14.880 --> 01:32:20.330
Then we have the Facilitation Squad, the Governance
Squad, the Protocol Squad, the Security Advisory

01:32:20.330 --> 01:32:22.900
Board, the newly proposed Working Groups.

01:32:22.900 --> 01:32:27.360
They’re expanding the DAO Committee from
3 to 5 members, but only after an election

01:32:27.360 --> 01:32:29.570
to replace one of the original 3.

01:32:29.570 --> 01:32:35.220
In trying to minimize human involvement they
have instead ballooned it, and, like, look

01:32:35.220 --> 01:32:37.679
at the participation numbers.

01:32:37.679 --> 01:32:42.540
Generously there’s a few hundred people
actively involved in the DAO in any capacity

01:32:42.540 --> 01:32:48.150
at all, and at this rate every single one
of them is going to be in at least one squad

01:32:48.150 --> 01:32:49.240
or work group.

01:32:49.240 --> 01:32:54.179
More realistically the couple dozen people
with the inclination to spend their free time

01:32:54.179 --> 01:32:59.300
role playing bureaucrats are going to be sitting
on the accountability squad that has oversight

01:32:59.300 --> 01:33:05.060
over the oversight work group that advises
the spending council on how much the accountability

01:33:05.060 --> 01:33:06.540
squad should be paid.

01:33:06.540 --> 01:33:11.170
Oh, and these bureaucrats are paid in MANA,
which as we’ve seen is a direct source of

01:33:11.170 --> 01:33:12.340
political power.

01:33:12.340 --> 01:33:18.330
Like, do you remember that time Yemel agreed
with Yemel that Yemel should be paid $150/hour

01:33:18.330 --> 01:33:19.330
in MANA?

01:33:19.330 --> 01:33:23.680
Would you be shocked to learn that Yemel,
who agreed with Yemel about paying Yemel $150

01:33:23.680 --> 01:33:27.810
an hour, is friends with Melich and Ordano
from back in the days when they all spent

01:33:27.810 --> 01:33:31.170
their time at a tech bro flop house they called
Casa Voltaire?

01:33:31.170 --> 01:33:35.389
And through all this bureaucratic garbage,
it’s easy to lose sight of the truth.

01:33:35.389 --> 01:33:41.050
The DAO has no actual authority.

01:33:41.050 --> 01:33:46.770
Decentraland isn’t “owned by its users”,
it's owned by its owners - the Foundation.

01:33:46.770 --> 01:33:51.520
While the Foundation cedes the copyright on
material submitted by users, the Terms of

01:33:51.520 --> 01:33:57.230
Use make it very clear that the Foundation
holds the copyright on the Decentraland part

01:33:57.230 --> 01:33:58.770
of Decentraland.

01:33:58.770 --> 01:34:03.750
As Catalina Goanta points out, this conflicts
with the rhetoric that Decentraland is owned

01:34:03.750 --> 01:34:10.100
by its users, when the most significant legal
right in this area is unambiguously owned

01:34:10.100 --> 01:34:11.639
by the Foundation.

01:34:11.639 --> 01:34:16.420
Even looking past that, can it really be said
that Decentraland is owned by its users when

01:34:16.420 --> 01:34:22.409
the DAO is currently being held hostage by
a dozen whales whose proportional voting rights

01:34:22.409 --> 01:34:24.150
dwarf their peers?

01:34:24.150 --> 01:34:28.770
Now, being a digital landlord doesn’t require
much engagement at all.

01:34:28.770 --> 01:34:33.429
I would hazard that whoever owns fifty identical
Decentraland homes plastered with For Sale

01:34:33.429 --> 01:34:39.420
advertisements isn’t really a Decentraland
user above the most literal threshold.

01:34:39.420 --> 01:34:43.900
I, on the other hand, am demonstrably a user
of Decentraland.

01:34:43.900 --> 01:34:49.040
Given how dead engagement is I feel pretty
confident in estimating that I spend far more

01:34:49.040 --> 01:34:54.020
hours per year actively looking around and
doing stuff in the world than the median LAND

01:34:54.020 --> 01:34:55.020
owner.

01:34:55.020 --> 01:35:00.179
But I am not an owner of Decentraland by virtue
of that time spent in the world, there is

01:35:00.179 --> 01:35:05.330
no mechanism that correlates the two, that
gives ownership to those who use it and strips

01:35:05.330 --> 01:35:08.060
ownership from those who idly hoard.

01:35:08.060 --> 01:35:14.520
The mechanism of ownership is not usage, it’s
ownership of LAND and MANA.&nbsp;

01:35:14.520 --> 01:35:18.460
The arc of Decentraland’s development is
dictated by the Foundation.

01:35:18.460 --> 01:35:23.739
They may occasionally ask the DAO what features
they’d want to see, or act on simple proposals

01:35:23.739 --> 01:35:30.690
for technical changes - but the DAO is not
Decentraland’s board of directors.

01:35:30.690 --> 01:35:35.659
“First, Decentraland is always working with
the community because we are a DAO.

01:35:35.659 --> 01:35:40.719
As a decentralized platform we are mostly
governed by the DAO.

01:35:40.719 --> 01:35:46.050
So we are not a corporation, so our roadmap
also operates differently because we always

01:35:46.050 --> 01:35:49.600
try to look into what the community is aiming.”

01:35:49.600 --> 01:35:54.330
Notice how she doesn’t say “we’re bound
by the decisions of the DAO, because they’re

01:35:54.330 --> 01:35:55.389
our bosses”.

01:35:55.389 --> 01:35:59.469
Instead the Foundation is “working with
the community” and “trying to look into

01:35:59.469 --> 01:36:01.449
where the community is aiming.”

01:36:01.449 --> 01:36:05.590
When discussing Decentraland’s prospective
value to actual businesses, the Foundation

01:36:05.590 --> 01:36:10.020
can’t say with a straight face that representatives
from Target need to get in touch with the

01:36:10.020 --> 01:36:14.040
DAO Facilitation Squad to outline the contents
of a proposal.

01:36:14.040 --> 01:36:19.469
At a certain point, all the talk of decentralized
ownership and political revolution needs to

01:36:19.469 --> 01:36:24.880
get put aside so Dolce &amp; Gabbana will actually
respond to the emails.

01:36:24.880 --> 01:36:28.730
In Line Goes Up, we all had a good laugh at
DAOs whose central product was undefined.

01:36:28.730 --> 01:36:32.830
“Are we a comic book, or a bi-monthly curated
box of snacks?”

01:36:32.830 --> 01:36:36.790
But to their credit - that is in keeping with
the philosophy of a DAO.

01:36:36.790 --> 01:36:42.199
In the case of Decentraland, the Foundation
came first and knows what it is, and what

01:36:42.199 --> 01:36:43.949
it wants to be.

01:36:43.949 --> 01:36:48.720
If the DAO were to vote for the project to
become a 2D Metroidvania set in a hypothetical

01:36:48.720 --> 01:36:54.699
fourth Punic War, in the visual style of NHL
‘94 - sorry, that isn’t happening.

01:36:54.699 --> 01:36:58.239
The Foundation acts autonomously whenever
it wants.

01:36:58.239 --> 01:37:02.880
When the Foundation implemented “the first
ATM in the metaverse”, they didn’t consult

01:37:02.880 --> 01:37:04.820
the DAO before starting development.

01:37:04.820 --> 01:37:06.820
And can you blame them?

01:37:06.820 --> 01:37:12.600
Can you imagine if, as a business, they were
bound by the whims of these losers?

01:37:12.600 --> 01:37:18.340
Despite the rhetoric, the Foundation isn’t
dependent on the DAO, in truth it's the opposite.

01:37:18.340 --> 01:37:21.530
The DAO can’t function without the Foundation.

01:37:21.530 --> 01:37:25.449
Not just in the obvious ways, but from top
to bottom.

01:37:25.449 --> 01:37:30.590
The 2021 proposal to go off-chain came from
the Executive Director of Decentraland.

01:37:30.590 --> 01:37:35.119
In the proposal, he nominated three individuals
to serve as the administrative Committee members

01:37:35.119 --> 01:37:39.710
- all of whom were working at the Foundation
at the time.

01:37:39.710 --> 01:37:44.610
The Decentraland DAO simply lacks the means
to function autonomously.

01:37:44.610 --> 01:37:50.070
Through lack of tools, unimaginable inequality,
general incompetence and a lack of authority,

01:37:50.070 --> 01:37:52.150
the DAO is impotent.

01:37:52.150 --> 01:38:01.369
So is it any wonder that their grant scheme
can turn $240,000 into the MetaGamiMall?

01:38:01.369 --> 01:38:07.830
What else could this system possibly produce?

01:38:07.830 --> 01:38:17.290
One of the most telling moments in the Decentraland
timeline is the June 2019 blog post announcing

01:38:17.290 --> 01:38:20.280
the switch from the Babylon engine to Unity.

01:38:20.280 --> 01:38:25.520
Not because of the engine change in and of
itself&nbsp; but because of this note: jumping

01:38:25.520 --> 01:38:26.520
added.

01:38:26.520 --> 01:38:30.970
Jumping puzzles, it’s important to note,
are functionally the single ludic interaction

01:38:30.970 --> 01:38:33.760
inherent to Decentraland’s mechanics.

01:38:33.760 --> 01:38:38.500
The only games that you see in the world with
any consistency, games that actually involve

01:38:38.500 --> 01:38:43.420
interacting with the space and not with a
two dimensional UI element, are scavenger

01:38:43.420 --> 01:38:45.790
hunts and jumping puzzles.

01:38:45.790 --> 01:38:50.199
Unless you’re headed to the gambling halls,
jumping on things is functionally the only

01:38:50.199 --> 01:38:57.070
thing to consistently do in Decentraland and
it was, this is important, an afterthought.

01:38:57.070 --> 01:39:03.730
2019, that’s four years of development before
jumping was added as basic functionality.

01:39:03.730 --> 01:39:08.710
Ari Meilich and Esteban Ordano are Argentinian
developers who met in a “hacker house”

01:39:08.710 --> 01:39:10.460
known as Casa Voltaire.

01:39:10.460 --> 01:39:15.140
Most of what we know about Casa Voltaire comes
from two online articles, one from 2017 and

01:39:15.140 --> 01:39:16.219
another from 2020.

01:39:16.219 --> 01:39:22.190
Casa Voltaire hit the sweet spot of anonymity,
there’s not really any contemporary information

01:39:22.190 --> 01:39:27.080
about what was going on there because it was
just a flop house for tech bros, but it left

01:39:27.080 --> 01:39:29.780
just enough of a footprint to become mythologised.

01:39:29.780 --> 01:39:35.909
“the community at the Voltaire Castle became
a worldwide legend a crazy hacker haven for

01:39:35.909 --> 01:39:37.219
cryptocurrency projects”

01:39:37.219 --> 01:39:41.420
As Ryan Vanzo describes it, the house was
a communal workspace where small start ups

01:39:41.420 --> 01:39:44.949
would operate in the house and share resources
and expertise.

01:39:44.949 --> 01:39:48.070
This was combined with a heady blend of smartboy
philosophy.

01:39:48.070 --> 01:39:52.540
Both of the previously mentioned articles
paint a picture of genius, citing the deep

01:39:52.540 --> 01:39:55.010
conversations that would take place within
the group.

01:39:55.010 --> 01:40:00.210
But, ya know, here’s the deep, moral dilemmas
Vanzo cites in his story.

01:40:00.210 --> 01:40:05.600
How should autonomous cars act in a situation
that would save the driver but kill a pedestrian?

01:40:05.600 --> 01:40:09.650
Who is liable if a bot malfunctions and causes
harm?

01:40:09.650 --> 01:40:14.560
What kind of neuro-security should we develop
to protect users of brain-to-brain interfaces?

01:40:14.560 --> 01:40:17.909
So, like, a high school introduction to ethics.

01:40:17.909 --> 01:40:20.060
One of those is literally the trolly problem.

01:40:20.060 --> 01:40:23.980
In this environment, and within the context
of Argentina’s political volatility, Ordano

01:40:23.980 --> 01:40:28.060
and Meilich conceived of Decentraland as a
bunch of things, principally a fulfillment

01:40:28.060 --> 01:40:33.250
of the fantasy of Snow Crash, The Matrix,
and, of course, Ready Player One.

01:40:33.250 --> 01:40:40.370
“The idea was basically whether we could
do a virtual world that was based on open

01:40:40.370 --> 01:40:47.580
standards and the blockchain and whether we
could encode the physics into... into a blockchain.

01:40:47.580 --> 01:40:57.020
Of course influenced by videos and movies
like The Matrix, Ready Player One, and Snow

01:40:57.020 --> 01:40:58.060
Crash.”

01:40:58.060 --> 01:41:03.310
But inside that was an inkling of the political,
the notion that this could be a test bed for

01:41:03.310 --> 01:41:09.420
political movements, for new economies, and,
ideally, liberation movements.

01:41:09.420 --> 01:41:14.070
Writing for Rest of the World, Leo Schwartz
and Lucia Cholakian Herrera describe it, skeptically,

01:41:14.070 --> 01:41:18.849
as “a petri dish for the ideals of democracy
and decentralization they championed, built

01:41:18.849 --> 01:41:23.570
on the premise that a virtual world controlled
by its own “citizens” could more effectively

01:41:23.570 --> 01:41:28.450
govern itself — and offer more stable investment
opportunities — than a real one governed

01:41:28.450 --> 01:41:30.170
by elites.”

01:41:30.170 --> 01:41:35.460
Of course in reality Decentraland is steered
exclusively by its own elites, people who

01:41:35.460 --> 01:41:40.310
bought in hard and early and have the voting
power to dismiss any challenge to their power,

01:41:40.310 --> 01:41:46.110
or literal project insiders who have the social
capital and access to the code base to simply

01:41:46.110 --> 01:41:48.190
end-run the will of the whales.

01:41:48.190 --> 01:41:54.270
Because Decentraland is not a petri dish,
sterilized and sealed off from our reality.

01:41:54.270 --> 01:42:00.840
In 2003, writers rushed to herald Second Life
as the vanguard of the future, not just a

01:42:00.840 --> 01:42:05.470
video game, but a new place that couldn’t
have existed before.

01:42:05.470 --> 01:42:10.790
Two years prior, Edward Castronova had argued
that Everquest and virtual worlds like it

01:42:10.790 --> 01:42:15.270
were important because the objects in them
were worth money.

01:42:15.270 --> 01:42:19.860
In response to Second Life, Castronova predicted
that in the future, humanity would pick the

01:42:19.860 --> 01:42:21.730
virtual world over the real.

01:42:21.730 --> 01:42:27.280
“The exodus of people from the real world,
from our normal daily life, of living rooms,

01:42:27.280 --> 01:42:32.880
cubicles and shopping malls, will create a
change in social climate that makes global

01:42:32.880 --> 01:42:36.480
warming look like a tempest in a teacup.”

01:42:36.480 --> 01:42:40.050
Philip Rosedale, a developer on Second Life,
went even further.

01:42:40.050 --> 01:42:44.099
“The real world will become like a museum
very soon.

01:42:44.099 --> 01:42:49.080
So it’ll be fantastically cool to go to
New York, but in the same way that it’s

01:42:49.080 --> 01:42:51.600
cool to go see the Mayan ruins.

01:42:51.600 --> 01:42:55.940
Because the big buildings will still be there,
but they’ll be covered in dust.

01:42:55.940 --> 01:42:58.570
Because no one will bother too much with them
anymore.”

01:42:58.570 --> 01:43:03.980
There’s just one tiny, almost inconsequential
problem here, just a little thing, we’ll

01:43:03.980 --> 01:43:09.150
talk about it in a moment here, but just maybe
keep in mind that there’s a small little

01:43:09.150 --> 01:43:10.150
issue.

01:43:10.150 --> 01:43:16.820
Johan Huizinga is known for his landmark anthropologic
work Homo Ludens, published in 1938.&nbsp;

01:43:16.820 --> 01:43:22.210
Huizinga was concerned with the interaction
between play and culture.

01:43:22.210 --> 01:43:25.460
And a key element of that was trying to define
‘play’.

01:43:25.460 --> 01:43:29.030
What is the difference between a fight and
play-fight?

01:43:29.030 --> 01:43:35.270
How is it that chalk on grass can produce
a boundary that people take seriously during

01:43:35.270 --> 01:43:36.719
a game of soccer?

01:43:36.719 --> 01:43:40.760
Huizinga proposed the concept of the “magic
circle”, which aims to describe how play

01:43:40.760 --> 01:43:44.230
space is delineated from the ordinary world.&nbsp;

01:43:44.230 --> 01:43:48.719
In a game of Dungeons &amp; Dragons, the rules
and instructions alone aren’t enough to

01:43:48.719 --> 01:43:55.070
represent the entire game world, it is “the
compound relationship of formal, social, dramatic

01:43:55.070 --> 01:43:56.570
and aesthetic elements.”

01:43:56.570 --> 01:44:01.780
Essentially, when you buy into the game, everything
comes together to create a kind of pocket

01:44:01.780 --> 01:44:07.110
dimension, a psychological bubble, where board
pieces, dice rolls and social interactions

01:44:07.110 --> 01:44:11.409
can take on unique meanings distinct from
reality.

01:44:11.409 --> 01:44:15.770
In a game of soccer, the players all agree
to obey the rules of the game and a social

01:44:15.770 --> 01:44:17.210
contract forms.

01:44:17.210 --> 01:44:22.050
Suddenly the ball, chalk, and their own limbs
take on properties prescribed to them in the

01:44:22.050 --> 01:44:28.250
player’s mind, boundaries become respectable
and in respecting them they become real.

01:44:28.250 --> 01:44:33.250
If, however, someone then karate chops the
opposing goalie in the throat, the bubble

01:44:33.250 --> 01:44:38.700
bursts - the contract has been broken, the
circle has been pierced, and the lines on

01:44:38.700 --> 01:44:42.090
the ground stop mattering real fast.

01:44:42.090 --> 01:44:46.311
In an honest engagement, where the fiction
is unavoidably present but elided for the

01:44:46.311 --> 01:44:51.750
sake of immersion we would call the circle
suspension of disbelief or kayfabe.

01:44:51.750 --> 01:44:56.949
Tom Boellstroff and Edward Castronova both
sought to push Second Life as a ‘real place,’

01:44:56.949 --> 01:45:00.770
separate from the real world, where genuine
research could be done.

01:45:00.770 --> 01:45:05.760
Boellstroff argued that Second Life was a
fixed site of culture, like a Pacific Island.

01:45:05.760 --> 01:45:11.880
Edward Castronova made a career out of arguing
that Second Life is a site of genuine economic

01:45:11.880 --> 01:45:12.880
activity.

01:45:12.880 --> 01:45:18.489
They, not in their words but in criticism,
present Second Life as existing within an

01:45:18.489 --> 01:45:26.150
impermeable magic circle, as like The Matrix,
somewhere you travel to, a place.

01:45:26.150 --> 01:45:33.030
Okay, but there’s just that one, tiny, minuscule
problem, and that’s that people who “live”

01:45:33.030 --> 01:45:39.020
in Second Life don’t actually live in Second
Life.

01:45:39.020 --> 01:45:44.469
You can eat an infinite amount of digital
food in virtual reality and you will still

01:45:44.469 --> 01:45:45.949
starve.

01:45:45.949 --> 01:45:52.040
But the metaverse both presumes and requires
that legitimacy of place and the real.

01:45:52.040 --> 01:45:57.280
Either the outright reification of Ryan Bolger,
or digital fashion being pushed as genuine

01:45:57.280 --> 01:46:02.360
property, it needs to be real in order to
be meaningful.

01:46:02.360 --> 01:46:07.900
So the authors of Decentraland, its creators
and its users, paint a magic circle around

01:46:07.900 --> 01:46:14.430
it with a narrative of inevitability, a narrative
of the metaverse, a narrative of a true, separate,

01:46:14.430 --> 01:46:19.270
new world that you will eventually move your
life into.

01:46:19.270 --> 01:46:24.880
Because if your neighbors aren’t going to
eventually be compelled to be here tomorrow,

01:46:24.880 --> 01:46:28.510
why would you ever want this today?

01:46:28.510 --> 01:46:32.219
Decentraland is, at every level, a collective
fairy tale.

01:46:32.219 --> 01:46:34.160
Just people playing pretend.

01:46:34.160 --> 01:46:39.480
“The feeling of these shows was that we
all performed a fashion week, rather than

01:46:39.480 --> 01:46:40.760
participating in it.”

01:46:40.760 --> 01:46:46.740
Whether it be the Pedigree Fosterverse scraping
data from adoptapet, users playing lawyer

01:46:46.740 --> 01:46:52.220
in their corporate offices or purporting to
be the future of news - Decentraland’s value

01:46:52.220 --> 01:46:55.780
to businesses is plainly absurd.

01:46:55.780 --> 01:46:59.500
And as we’ve seen, even its decentralized
premise is a fantasy.

01:46:59.500 --> 01:47:04.670
The DAO has no authority and is comically
hapless - content to play politics.

01:47:04.670 --> 01:47:08.740
All the while pretending they have a stake
in a billion dollar product.

01:47:08.740 --> 01:47:13.230
And it’s not enough to convince themselves,
they need to convince you.

01:47:13.230 --> 01:47:16.540
So that is what they do by any means necessary.

01:47:16.540 --> 01:47:22.880
They will pander, mislead, outright lie - whatever
it takes for you to buy into their narrative.

01:47:22.880 --> 01:47:28.159
Because this only makes sense from inside
the circle.

01:47:28.159 --> 01:47:32.239
“What if you could own the virtual world?

01:47:32.239 --> 01:47:37.080
Create, develop, and trade without limits.

01:47:37.080 --> 01:47:40.820
Make genuine connections, and earn real money.”

01:47:40.820 --> 01:47:45.890
Decentraland is a farce and a tragedy, it
is painted into a corner by a combination

01:47:45.890 --> 01:47:52.090
of ineptitude and inherently bad ideas, and
it cannot escape its fundamental being.&nbsp;

01:47:52.090 --> 01:47:58.230
Whatever other ideals are spit out, whatever
rhetoric about liberation or political experimentation

01:47:58.230 --> 01:48:05.010
is employed, the simple fact that it was materially
born as a pre-sale of lots of "land" based

01:48:05.010 --> 01:48:10.280
on a fiction of people "moving in" sets off
a chain of decisions and incentives about

01:48:10.280 --> 01:48:16.119
design and functionality that bind it, forever,
to being little more than a fantasy real estate

01:48:16.119 --> 01:48:29.719
scheme, an endless world of uniquely scarce
dead malls.

01:48:29.719 --> 01:48:34.389
I can’t use that right now.

01:48:34.389 --> 01:48:40.380
I can’t use that right now.

01:48:40.380 --> 01:48:47.080
I can’t use that right now.

01:48:47.080 --> 01:48:49.719
Thank you.

01:48:49.719 --> 01:48:53.119
I can’t use that right now.

01:48:53.119 --> 01:48:54.670
I can’t use that right now.

01:48:54.670 --> 01:48:57.210
Mighty kind of you.

01:48:57.210 --> 01:49:04.949
I can’t use that right now.

01:49:04.949 --> 01:49:07.780
I can’t use that right now.

01:49:07.780 --> 01:49:08.780
[gunshot]

01:49:08.780 --> 01:49:14.420
Oh my god, what did you do!?

01:49:14.420 --> 01:49:19.840
I can’t use that right now.

